.\" Man page for git-edit. Kept in sync with the script's USAGE() and README.
.\" Auto-found via man's PATH derivation (<repo>/man/man1) once the repo is on
.\" PATH, so `git edit --help` / `man git-edit` work with no install step.
.TH GIT-EDIT 1 "2026-11-13" "git-edit" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git\-edit \- edit, reword, drop, squash, reorder, split or fold past commits
.PP
Without a terminal, \fBgit edit \-h\fR prints the usage, and
\fBgit edit \-h\fR \fItopic\fR... this page's entries for those flags or
sections, as plain text.
.B \-\-help
opens this page in
.BR man ,
which emits backspace\-overstruck text.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-e\fR | \fB\-\-edit\fR] \fIcommit\fR               # edit a commit (default)
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-M\fR | \fB\-\-reword\fR] [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR | \fB\-\-subject\fR \fIline\fR] \fIcommit\fR
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-s\fR | \fB\-\-squash\fR | \fB\-d\fR | \fB\-\-drop\fR] \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-s=\fR\fItarget\fR] \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-s=auto\fR [\fIcommit\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR} [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB\-\-tree=\fR\fItree\-ish\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-split=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-reorder\fR \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-move=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fIoldest\fR\fB..\fR\fInewest\fR}... {\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-at=\fR\fIcut\fR}
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-onto=\fR\fIupstream\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR]    # replant this branch onto \fIupstream\fR
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-land=\fR\fIbranch\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]   # land \fIbranch\fR's commits on this branch
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-exec\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-at=\fR\fIcut\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-commit\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-at=\fR\fIcut\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR} \fB\-\-whole\fR [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR {\fB\-\-commit \-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR] | \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR}} [\fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR] {\fB\-\-edits=\fR\fIjson\fR | \fB\-\-patch=\fR\fIfile\fR | \fB\-\-put=\fR\fIpath\fR\fB=\fR\fIfile\fR | \fB\-\-rm=\fR\fIpath\fR | \fB\-\-chmod=\fR\fIpath\fR\fB=\fR{\fB+x\fR|\fB\-x\fR}}... [[\fB\-\-whole\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-commit\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-tree=\fR\fIcommit\fR [\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-continue\fR | \fB\-\-skip\fR | \fB\-\-abort\fR | \fB\-\-undo\fR | \fB\-\-status\fR | \fB\-\-carry\fR[\fB=\fR\fIsha\fR] | \fB\-\-selftest\fR[\fB=\fR\fIids\fR] [\fB\-\-jobs\fR[\fB=\fR\fIn\fR]] | \fB\-\-version\fR
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-h\fR [\fItopic\fR...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git edit
is a wrapper around
.BR git\-rebase (1)
for editing, reordering, dropping, merging, splitting, and folding earlier
commits. It is hardened for use by AI coding agents and parallel sessions:
.IP \(bu 3
Every ref move is an atomic compare\-and\-swap (3\-arg
.BR "git update\-ref" ),
so a concurrent commit from another worktree or session is refused, never
overwritten.
.IP \(bu 3
The plumbing modes
.RB ( \-M ,
.BR \-S ,
a pathspec
.BR \-\-split )
and the isolated modes
.RB ( \-\-amend\-into ,
a content
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-reorder ,
.BR \-\-exec ,
.BR \-\-commit ,
.BR \-\-land )
build their result without touching the main working tree \(em a run brings
the checkout it started in along only once it lands, an agent's as a
person's (see
.BR "THE CHECKOUT" )
\(em and a fold consumes staged changes only as its final CAS lands, so a
failure or
.B \-\-abort
leaves them exactly as they were, with nothing to roll back.
.IP \(bu 3
An edit, drop or squash, and a land's replay, also runs in its own temporary
worktree, or the
.B \-C
path, reading the config the branch's checkout reads \(em an
.B onbranch
include's identity or signing key, a relative
.B core.hooksPath
whose untracked hooks only the checkout has, as husky's \(em through an
.B includeIf
only this repository's git dirs read. At a terminal \(em stdin and stdout a
TTY, with none of
.BR CI ,
.B CLAUDECODE
or
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
set \(em a pause asks rather than exits. See
.BR "TERMINAL RUNS" .
.IP \(bu 3
Output carries blank\-line padding and colour only when stdout is a terminal.
Piped or captured it comes out tight \(em the same lines, unpadded, each from
column zero \(em so even a
.B "| tail -3"
reaches the trailer and the line naming the commit the run touched \(em every
mode but \fB\-\-exec\fR, whose command is yours and has no single target.
Nothing above those two is guaranteed: a stat block or path list grows with
the commit. A name reaches further than a count \(em
.B "| grep -E -B2 '^(Verif|Tip\ tree|Trees|Note:\ tip\ tree|git-edit:)'"
answers what landed, what checked it and whether the trees came out as they
were (\fBTip tree identical\fR, \fBTrees unchanged\fR) or not (a fold's
\fBNote: tip tree differs from the staged result\fR), wherever the gate's
summary sits \(em its \fB\-B2\fR keeping the two lines above each match, where
a refusal names its fix. A run that declined a gate says so too \(em
.B "Verify skipped \(en --no-verify was passed, so `npm test` never ran"
\(em so silence means only that the repo configured no gate.
.IP \(bu 3
Every rewrite reports its old\(->new map the way git does: notes are carried
across it (honoring \fBnotes.rewriteRef\fR and \fBnotes.rewriteMode\fR, a no\-op
when unset), and \fBpost\-rewrite\fR hooks run with the same pairs on stdin
\(em a hook file and one defined in config alike, through
.B git hook run
(git 2.40+, where older releases run the file alone). It happens once, when
the rewrite lands: the rebase\-based modes hold git's own delivery back until
then, so an aborted or refused rewrite is never announced, and a commit the
run makes along the way (a fold's \fBfixup!\fR) is never listed. A
.B git rebase \-\-continue
typed into a pause worktree that finishes the rebase lets git deliver it
there, and the landing does not repeat it. A squash maps every member onto the commit that absorbed
them; a split maps the original onto the remainder, which keeps its message
and tree.
.IP \(bu 3
Only the branch being rewritten moves. Another branch or a tag left pointing
into the rewritten span is named instead, with the command re\-pointing it to
its counterpart \(em the commit the rewrite's own record maps it to (a split's
remainder or a fold's target, say, flagged where its change differs), else one
matched as a stale \fIcommit\fR is: only a commit carrying the same change
counts, a subject match alone offered as a guess to check first. One on a
commit the run dropped is named as such, with no command, and one on a commit
it squashed away names the commit it went into. The default branch (failing
one, the main worktree's branch) and a tag it still holds are that branch's
own, so
rewriting any other branch or a detached HEAD \(em one made from it and
rewritten below its tip or tag, say \(em leaves that branch unnamed and offers
no command for the tag. A branch or tag only a backup or another side branch
holds is named as any other.
.B rebase.updateRefs
is not applied: git moves such a branch as its rebase completes, before the
result is verified or lands, so an aborted or refused rewrite would leave the
branch rewritten. A branch checked out in a worktree is left for that worktree
to move, as git leaves it \(em but named all the same, with its counterpart
and no command.
.IP
A tag that
.B edit.followTag
names follows the rewrite instead, once the run lands (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.IP \(bu 3
A \fIcommit\fR that an earlier rewrite has orphaned is resolved to its current
identity when one commit carries the identical diff, and otherwise refused with
its likely counterpart named \(em so a stale SHA never silently no\-ops. A match
that is itself pushed is named but never substituted, not even under
.BR \-\-allow\-pushed ,
which consents to rewriting the commit you named, not one inferred for you.
Candidates are pruned by author date, which every rewrite preserves \(em where
a single timestamp holds more than 100 commits, resolution only names a
suggestion.
.IP \(bu 3
Merge topology is preserved: the plumbing modes rebuild merge commits with
their parents faithfully substituted, and the rebase\-based modes refuse a
span containing a merge rather than silently flattening it.
.PP
Every run ends with a machine\-readable status trailer (see
.BR "OUTCOME TRAILER" ),
refuses to rewrite commits already on a remote unless
.B \-\-allow\-pushed
is given, and records its ref move in the branch reflog and a journal.
.PP
It needs git 2.31 or later and refuses an older one outright. Every mode needs a
checked\-out branch but
.BR \-M ,
.B \-S
and a split by pathspec.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-version
answer regardless.
.SH MODES
A mode or option that takes one value \(em
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-onto ,
.BR \-\-land ,
.BR \-s= ,
.BR \-\-after ,
.BR \-\-before ,
.BR \-\-tree ,
.BR \-\-text ,
.BR \-\-subject ,
.BR \-\-verify ,
.BR \-\-edits ,
.BR \-\-base ,
.BR \-C / \-\-dir ,
.BR \-\-selftest ,
.B \-\-jobs
or
.B \-\-carry
\(em refuses to be given twice, where only one value would count.
.B \-\-move
alone repeats, each time naming more of what it moves. A value given apart
that is a flag, or the
.B \-\-
ending them, refuses as a value gone missing; glue one on with
.B =
where it is meant. A long option's value is given apart or after
.BR = ,
so a word running on past an option's name, as
.BR \-\-text\-file ,
is an unknown option. A \fIcommit\fR may be
a range \fIA\fB..\fIB\fR, which includes both its ends, unlike git's own. One
end an earlier rewrite orphaned resolves as a single \fIcommit\fR does; with
both orphaned, each commit of the range as named resolves alone, as the span
between their current identities can hold a commit moved in since.
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-edit
.nf
\fBgit edit\fR \fIcommit\fR                                      # pause with a worktree at it, to edit files there
\fBgit edit \-\-continue\fR [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR | \fB\-\-subject\fR \fIline\fR]  # amend, replay and land it \(em or \fB\-\-abort\fR
.fi
.IP
Edit \fIcommit\fR (default). Naming the mode refuses a second \fIcommit\fR,
which the bare form reads as a squash.
.IP
The pause holds a worktree checked out at the commit, named by a
.B paused
trailer. Edit files there (clean of any main\-checkout WIP), then run
.B git edit \-\-continue
(with
.B \-\-text
to also reword, or
.B \-\-subject
to replace the subject alone), or press Enter at a terminal's prompt. That
amends, replays the descendants, prints the net history change and
CAS\-applies, the branch moving only then.
.IP
The replay resolves its own stops as a fold's does (see
.BR \-\-amend\-into ),
the change from the paused commit to what it became playing the fold's: a
later commit whose parent as replayed differs by that change alone takes it
in its own content wherever it holds each old text exactly once, on the
continue that amends and on every resume after a stop resolved by hand.
.IP
Once the edit lands, the main checkout comes along as after any landing \(em
see
.BR "THE CHECKOUT" .
Where it can't, the index entries the move stranded are re\-synced, and the
paths still holding the pre\-edit content are named, with the one
.B \-\-carry
that brings them along, never a restore.
.IP
A path carrying further uncommitted edits is named apart. Where those edits
sit on the pre\-edit content, it is named as taking the rewrite back if
committed whole. Where the rewrite renamed it, it is named beside its new
path, the edits left untracked at the old one. Where the checkout has no file
there, deleted or never moved in from the old path of an earlier run's
rename, it is named as missing.
.IP
Named apart as well are the untracked files left in a directory the rewrite
renamed whole, each with its place under the new one, and those right in one
it renamed only in part, which stay. So are a directory standing where a file
lands, and a path under a symlink or a file of the caller's. A case\-only
rename on a filesystem that ignores case reads as its new name alone.
.IP
A path holding the new content under another mode, such as an executable bit
lost to a file moved into place, is named apart too, with both modes and the
.B chmod
that matches the commit.
.IP
A command printed over more than ten paths reads them through
.BR xargs (1)
from a file the run writes and names, so it runs at any count, the list
beside it naming ten and how many more. A list no command covers names every
path. A name holding a newline is spelled as
\fB$\(aq\&...\(aq\fR spells it, so a list keeps to its line. A fold and
.B \-\-exec
bring the checkout along the same way.
.IP
The amend stages the worktree wholesale, so a scratch file left there joins
the commit. Any untracked file it absorbs is named before the amend.
.IP
Commits made at the stop stand in for the amend, and what followed
\fIcommit\fR replays onto them. One amending \fIcommit\fR is named as the
edited commit. Ones made on top of it are named as added after it, and
\fIcommit\fR stays as it was. A worktree reset there to a commit that
followed \fIcommit\fR stands in for nothing and refuses. So does an edit that
empties \fIcommit\fR, and that refusal names
.B \-\-abort
and then
.B \-d
to drop it instead.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-reword
.nf
\fBgit edit \-M \-\-subject=\fR\fIline\fR \fIcommit\fR  # replace the subject, the body kept
\fBgit edit \-M \-\-text=\fR\fImsg\fR \fIcommit\fR      # replace the whole message
\fBgit edit \-M \-\-text \-\fR < \fIfile\fR          # many at once, from \fB\-\-\- \fR\fIcommit\fR records
.fi
.IP
Alter only \fIcommit\fR's message, via plumbing \(em no checkout, stash, rebase,
or worktree, the working tree and tip tree unchanged.
Descendants of a linear history are rebuilt in one \fBgit replay\fR pass (git
2.44+), kept only once every rebuilt commit is proven to carry its original's
tree, author and message \(em otherwise, as under a merge, one
\fBcommit\-tree\fR per commit. Either way a commit only rebuilt keeps its
message and author line byte for byte, as does the message a plumbing squash
keeps, and the rebuild ends at the tip the run read. Where the branch moved
meanwhile the run refuses, naming the move, with nothing applied \(em run it
again on the new tip. A message typed in an editor for the run
.RB ( \-M ,
.B \-S \-m
or
.BR "\-s \-m" )
is kept in a file in the repository's git directory, which the refusal names
with the rerun taking it through
.BR "\-\-text \-" .
.IP
Drop \fIcommit\fR and pass \fB\-\-text \-\fR to reword many commits at once,
reading \fB\-\-\-\fR \fIcommit\fR records from stdin \(em a header naming a
commit, then its new message, the shape
.B git log \-\-format='\-\-\- %h%n%B'
emits. One walk rebuilds the span under a single compare\-and\-swap; it stays
plumbing (every tree reused verbatim), skips a record whose message is
unchanged, and is all\-or\-nothing \(em an unreachable,
pushed, empty, or duplicated target aborts the batch before anything is written.
A stale SHA resolves by patch id. A commit whose own message contains a
.B \-\-\-
line is refused (that line is the record separator) \(em reword it singly.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-drop
Delete one or more \fIcommit\fRs or ranges. The dropped content leaves the
checkout with the commit, edits on it kept, the run naming the paths it took
out with the way back \(em
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
while it is the last run, else
.BR "git show \fIold\-tip\fB:\fIpath\fR" .
Dropping every commit of the branch refuses.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-squash
Merge \fIcommit\fRs into the oldest of them (or into \fItarget\fR with
.BR \-s= \fItarget\fR),
a single \fIcommit\fR into its parent.
Commits older than \fItarget\fR replay right before it, in their order, as it
was built on them \(em the fold keeping \fItarget\fR's authorship, and its
message where \fB\-\-text\fR, \fB\-m\fR or a marker brings none.
It takes the plumbing path when the selection is a contiguous, merge\-free
range into its oldest commit.
Given neither \fB\-\-text\fR nor \fB\-m\fR, the message follows the markers it
folds in, as git's autosquash does: an \fBamend!\fR commit naming the target
takes the place of its message, a \fBsquash!\fR one adds its body.
A squash ends on the tip's content \(em one whose resolutions add up to
anything else refuses to land, naming the commits the differing lines came
from or were lost at, and keeps its replay paused for one of them to be
reworked there before \fB\-\-continue\fR, or for \fB\-\-abort\fR and a redo
\(em rerere forgetting what it recorded at the stop named.
Its replay resolves its own stops where every commit it folds touching a
conflicted file only replaces or adds lines it can place, as a fold of staged
changes does (see
.BR \-\-amend\-into ):
the commit a stop builds takes the replacements of the commits folded at or
below it, bar those it holds already, at a folded commit's own step its own,
wherever it holds each old text, and a line beside each line added, exactly
once, and a resume goes on so, bar a file a resolution by hand staged
otherwise.
.IP
With
.BR \-s=auto ,
each \fIcommit\fR goes into the commit its \fBfixup!\fR, \fBamend!\fR or
\fBsquash!\fR marker names, every pair in one replay from the oldest target,
placed as git's autosquash places them. Below the marker on the first\-parent
line, the target's whole subject names it, else a SHA or other name, among the
unpushed commits as in git's own pass, then among the pushed; its opening words
name it only where neither answers anywhere, and the run says so. A commit
named outright without a marker that no marker names either, a marker naming
no commit below it or several alike, and one whose target is pushed (without
\fB\-\-allow\-pushed\fR) refuse the run, each named, as does one changing or
removing a file a commit after its target adds, that commit named (without
\fB\-\-allow\-new\-path\fR). A marker naming no commit below it because its
target was reworded since, or a SHA it names rewritten, names the commit that
became of it. A commit a range brings
without a marker stays where it is, and may be a target \(em one starting at
the tip a land began from takes that commit too. Each target takes the message
its markers ask for, so \fB\-\-text\fR and \fB\-m\fR refuse, and the gate checks
the commit each target became.
.IP
A bare
.B \-s=auto
takes the caller's own pending fixups: the unpushed \fBfixup!\fR, \fBamend!\fR
and \fBsquash!\fR commits on the branch's first\-parent line that the journal
names its
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
label as landing \(em to a caller with none, those an unlabeled run or no
git\-edit run landed. A commit is known there by its author, author date,
subject and the paths it changes, which a replay keeps, so a landing since
that gave it a new SHA still leaves the run that brought it in: the first
since it was made to bring it onto the branch. A run that gives one a new
subject or paths \(em a reword, a fold into it, the half a split inserts \(em
brings it in anew, as its caller's. Another caller's are
left to it, as squashing a fix takes its context, and
.B \-\-status
lists them. With none of its own the run ends
.BR "ok \(en \fIref\fP unchanged, no pending fixups of yours" ;
one it can't take refuses the run as above, naming the
.B \-s=auto
taking the rest alone, and for one whose target is pushed, the reword that
keeps it a commit of its own.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-resquash
Strict squash: plumbing only; errors instead of falling back to a rebase.
.TP
.B \-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR|\fBauto\fR} [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB\-\-tree=\fR\fItree\-ish\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-amend\-into=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-whole \-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...                   # fold these files whole from the checkout
\fBgit edit \-\-amend\-into=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-edits=\fR\fIjson\fR [\fB\-\-whole \-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...]  # amid another's edits in the same files
\fBgit add\fR \fIpath\fR... \fB&& git edit \-\-amend\-into=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...      # fold what you staged, scoped to it
\fBgit edit \-\-amend\-into=auto\fR ...                                     # into the unpushed commit owning the lines
\fBgit edit \-\-amend\-into=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR ...                       # reword it in the same run
.fi
.IP
Fold the staged changes into \fIsha\fR in an isolated worktree, as a fixup
placed beside it, leaving unstaged edits untouched.
.IP
A \fIpathspec\fR narrows the fold to those staged paths and leaves the rest
staged \(em worth it wherever the index may hold more than you mean to fold,
as a parallel session can stage into it between your
.BR git\-add (1)
and this call. A \fIpathspec\fR holding pattern characters, or opening with a
.B :
that no magic follows, is that path alone when it names a staged path, a
staged removal included (or, with
.BR \-\-tree ,
a path the tree holds or removes). As a pattern,
.I app/[id].tsx
would also fold
.IR app/i.tsx ,
and git reads
.I :a.txt
as
.IR a.txt .
One naming no path stays as git reads it, and magic given outright
(\fB:(\fR...\fB)\fR, \fB:/\fR, \fB:!\fR, \fB:^\fR) stays as given.
.IP
.B auto
targets the unpushed commit that last touched the folded \fIlines\fR (per
.BR git\-blame ),
whether staged here or composed by the inputs,
.B \-\-whole
or
.BR \-\-tree .
It falls back to the newest commit touching the staged paths only where the
change adds lines and so attributes to nothing. It refuses when the evidence
points at several commits or a pushed one, or where a submodule's pointer is
among the changes, which no line attributes; name its commit instead.
.IP
A conflict on the fold's final step resolves itself, as a reorder's does:
that step's result is the staged tree, provable before anything is committed,
so only earlier steps pause \(em and the final one where that resolution would
leave its commit empty. A pause carries any name but one holding a newline; a fold
that would pause over one refuses instead, naming it, with nothing landed.
.IP
The completion summary names the folded paths, ten at most and how many
more, verifies what landed, counts commits a conflict resolution left empty
(the rebase drops them) and notes a tip that differs from the staged result
\(em the pre\-op tip plus the staged changes. A fold that would leave history unchanged is refused, the staged
changes left intact \(em the case where every staged hunk is already carried
by the target, or its fixup was stepped over by hand with
.BR "git rebase \-\-skip" .
.IP
A staged path absent at \fIsha\fR that a later commit introduces is refused,
naming that commit, as it would replay as an add/add conflict.
.B \-\-allow\-new\-path
overrides. A removal folded there is refused too, with no override. A path new to the entire span backfills
cleanly and passes with a note.
.IP
A path renamed since folds onto its old name instead, wherever git's rename
detection pairs the two as the replay's own merge will, and a note names the
old path. Detection misses the pair where later edits outgrew the old
content. If the commit the path arrives in renamed it, the refusal of a path
the fold keeps names the old path at \fIsha\fR and that commit, and the edit
pause that changes the file under its old name:
.BI "git edit " sha \fR,
the change made to the old path in its worktree, then
.BR "git edit \-\-continue" .
.IP
A flipped file mode is refused as well \(em a fold rarely means one, the
usual cause being a literal mode typed into
.BR "git update\-index \-\-cacheinfo" ,
and no diffstat shows it.
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
overrides.
.IP
The guard holds through the replay: at a conflict stop,
.B \-\-continue
refuses a staged resolution leaving a path at a mode neither the replayed
commit nor its new parent carries, naming the
.B chmod
and
.B git add
that restore it, worktree and index both. The landing also refuses a tip
carrying a mode change nothing asked for, naming the rebuilt commit that
brought it in.
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
on the continue keeps either.
.IP
.B \-\-tree
takes the fold's content from \fItree\-ish\fR instead of the index \(em for
the checkout whose shared index is itself the hazard, a parallel session's
uncommitted edits sitting in the very file. Compose it apart from that index:
seed a private one from
.B HEAD
.RB ( "GIT_INDEX_FILE=... git read\-tree HEAD" ),
place the blobs at
.BR HEAD 's
modes, and
.BR write\-tree .
Handed over as a commit on
.BR HEAD ,
it pins the tip it was composed on, and a tip that moved since is refused
\(em a bare tree
carries no such pin, and is checked against other callers' landings instead.
The shared index is neither read nor written \(em it comes along with the
checkout as after any landing, an entry another caller staged kept.
.IP
A fold of what is staged is guarded as
.B \-\-commit
is, reading the staging once for the guard and the fold alike: staged before
another caller's landing, it would take that landing back where the fold
rebuilds those files as staged, so it refuses, naming the
.B "git restore \-\-staged"
that takes what landed instead, or the
.B \-\-carry
that merges what landed into the checkout and the
.B "git add"
that stages the files again \(em a file the landing renamed unstaged first,
as a carry leaves a staged old path where it is, and staged again under its
new name. That
.B "git add"
is named only for a file the checkout holds just as staged; one holding more,
another caller's uncommitted lines say, has its staging rebuilt alone instead,
what landed merged into the staged copy by the
.B git merge\-file
and
.B "git update\-index \-\-cacheinfo"
named, under the entry's own mode, and one whose staging conflicts with what
landed is to be replayed with
.B \-\-edits
or
.BR \-\-patch ,
then unstaged. For an executable bit alone, it names the
.B "git update\-index \-\-chmod"
that sets it on the staging, which staging the file again would not.
Staging or a
.B \-\-tree
carrying conflict markers its tip lacks refuses as a file taken whole does,
and a
.B \-\-tree
spelling a name two ways a case\-insensitive filesystem reads as one refuses as
.B \-\-commit
does.
.IP
.B \-\-text
also rewords \fIsha\fR in the same run \(em
.BR git\-commit (1)
.BR \-\-amend " " \-m
semantics. The reword is plumbing and runs before the fixup, so both land
under one ref update.
.TP
.B \-\-split=\fIsha\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-split=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...  # those changes into their own commit beneath \fIsha\fR
\fBgit edit \-\-split=\fR\fIsha\fR [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR]                 # pause to author the split point, for halves sharing a file
.fi
.IP
Split \fIsha\fR in two: the \fIpathspec\fR\-matched changes move into their
own commit. Plumbing only \(em no conflicts possible. The \fIpathspec\fR must
match a nonempty, proper subset of the commit's changes \(em none or all is
refused rather than composing an empty half. One holding pattern characters, or opening with a
.B :
that no magic follows, that names a path in
the commit is that path alone, as under
.BR \-\-amend\-into .
Where the commit turns a file into a directory at one path, the
\fIpathspec\fR names both sides or neither \(em one taking the directory's
files without the file they replace is refused, naming it. A \fIsha\fR an
earlier rewrite staled resolves to its current identity, as in every other mode.
.IP
With no \fIpathspec\fR \(em for halves sharing a file, which no pathspec can
name apart \(em the split pauses in an isolated worktree holding the commit's
content, to author the split point there: edit it back to what the first
commit should leave, then
.B git edit \-\-continue
(with
.B \-\-text
if not given up front) commits that tree, re\-commits \fIsha\fR's tree on top
and replays the descendants. It refuses when either half would be empty, when
the worktree touches a path \fIsha\fR never did, when it carries a file mode
neither \fIsha\fR nor its parent does (a file written anew into the worktree
comes back without its executable bit, which the remainder would flip back
\(em
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
keeps it), or when \fIsha\fR was rewritten mid\-pause.
.IP
Both forms insert the new commit beneath \fIsha\fR, which stays above it with
its tree, message and identity \(em so
.B \-\-text
always names the inserted commit, and \fIsha\fR keeps its subject whether or
not it still fits.
.TP
.B \-\-reorder \fIcommit\fR...
Reorder a contiguous span into the order the arguments give, oldest\-first,
in an isolated worktree; the branch ref moves only at the end. A conflict on
the final step resolves itself: a reorder preserves the tree, so the pre\-op
blobs are the answer, proven by the staged tree before anything is committed.
An unprovable case pauses as usual.
.IP
A reorder only moves commits, so one ending on another tree refuses to land,
naming the files \(em after a resume whose resolutions changed them, or a
clean replay too, as a revert moved before what it reverts no longer takes it
back.
.IP
Nor may a stop's resolution take in what a commit the new order puts after
it changes. A commit moved below one it builds on, its stop resolved to what
it holds, empties that one into it. The landing refuses, naming it, and
offers to squash the two instead, or, where other commits stood between
them, the move right after it. A stop whose pre\-op content would leave its
own commit no change there says so at the pause, naming that same way on
rather than the pre\-op content. A later commit that, rebuilt, still rewrites
the line the moved one changes lands, its change rebased onto that one's.
.IP
A rebase failing with nothing to resolve (a
.B pre\-rebase
hook's veto, a failed signature) refuses with git's own error and pauses
nothing, as it does for
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-s ,
.B \-\-onto
and
.BR \-\-land ,
and a placed
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-exec .
One failing as a resume replays, on a later commit or in an edit's replay of
the commits after it, keeps the pause: once signing works, the next
.B git edit \-\-continue
makes that commit again, as git left its change staged with nothing to commit
it. A git before 2.42.1 commits that change itself on the continue, and a
reorder then stops once more on the pick made again, come out empty \(em one
more
.B git edit \-\-continue
takes it past.
.TP
.B \-\-move=\fIsha\fR {\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIanchor\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIanchor\fR}
Reposition one commit directly after (or before) \fIanchor\fR, in either
direction, deriving the minimal span and its new order and running them
through \fB\-\-reorder\fR's machinery. A commit already in position is a clean no\-op.
With
.B \-\-at=\fIcut\fR
in place of the anchor, the commits land as the cut's last ones.
.IP
Its replay resolves its own stops as a fold's does (see
.BR \-\-amend\-into ),
the moved commits' changes playing the fold's, on a resume too: at a moved
commit's own step its change is made in what now precedes it, and a commit
it moved across makes that change in its own content, or takes it back where
it moved past it \(em each wherever the file holds every old text exactly
once. A stop those leave it resolves by line identity, file by file, where
the file is in a plain content conflict \(em or one a commit still to come
adds and the moved span never renames: moved later, a crossed commit keeps
its own edits, beside the moved lines too, and lines it inserts, between two
moved ones too, while its edits to the moved lines, or to a file the moved
commit adds, land with the commit that wrote them; moved earlier, the moved
commit's edits to lines a crossed commit wrote land with that one. Lines each
edited in place go their own ways, and a line both edited in place, at words
an untouched one lies between \(em each run of letters and digits and each
other character a word too, as a regex holds no space \(em or by one commit
only re\-indented, in a hunk replacing more lines too, stops the run there for
the hand, as no carry keeps that edit its own commit's; a hunk rewrapping
moved lines with older ones lands whole with the moved commit while its lines
are at least half of those the hunk replaces. Where no line identity is sure,
a file the one commit still to come changing it only adds a block of lines
to has that block taken back out of the stop by its content \(em each way it
aligns found there once, taking out the same lines \(em and the rest merged
as git would, that commit's own step adding the block again. The run names
each edit that landed with another commit, and the step taking it stops to
make it. The step that must build
the tree the moved lines built where they stood \(em moved later, the last
moved commit's; moved earlier, the crossed commit they followed \(em takes
that tree once it proves to be its own change and the edits carried to it
atop what precedes it, content no line holds compared whole: a resolution of
the run's own that broke that refuses the run, nothing applied and what rerere
recorded of it forgotten, for a rerun under
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_SELF_RESOLVE
to resolve by hand, while after a stop resolved by hand it pauses there,
naming what the step takes in beyond, and a continue takes it as staged. A hunk mixing fewer
moved lines than older ones, a line placed where nothing ordered it against
what stands there \(em bar lines abutting one end of a run a single commit
rewrote, which go that end's side of its old lines \(em an alignment whose
least forms read differently \(em none sure before git 2.50 \(em or a crossed
commit whose whole change would go elsewhere pauses that file as before. A plain
.B \-\-reorder
pauses as before.
.TP
.B \-\-move=\fIa\fR \-\-move=\fIb\fR... {\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIanchor\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIanchor\fR}
Given more than once, move every commit it names to \fIanchor\fR as one
block, in history order whatever order they were named in. Commits between
them stay put, so on a branch other sessions commit to, this form moves
exactly what it names. The anchor may lie below, above or between them, not
among them. The block moves in one replay.
.TP
.B \-\-move=\fIoldest\fB..\fInewest\fR
A value may name a run of adjacent commits instead, both ends included as in
every git edit range, unlike git's own \fIA\fB..\fIB\fR; they may come either
way round, and an open end means
.BR HEAD ,
so \fIoldest\fB..\fR takes everything from \fIoldest\fR up. The run is every
commit between its ends, whoever made it \(em where other sessions commit to
the branch, name each commit instead, since a commit landed in between, or on
top of an open end, would move with it. Ends an earlier rewrite orphaned
resolve as a positional range's do: one alone as a single \fIcommit\fR, both
by resolving each commit of the run as named.
.TP
.B \-\-onto=\fIupstream\fR
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-onto=\fR\fIupstream\fR               # the fork point read from \fIupstream\fR's reflog
\fBgit edit \-\-onto=\fR\fIupstream\fR \fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR  # the fork named: replay only what follows \fIsha\fR
\fBgit edit \-\-continue\fR | \fB\-\-skip\fR | \fB\-\-abort\fR   # after a pause
.fi
.IP
Replant the current branch onto \fIupstream\fR, deriving the fork point:
where
.B "git rebase \-\-onto"
needs the old base spelled out, this recovers it from \fIupstream\fR's reflog
via
.BR "git merge\-base \-\-fork\-point" ,
even where a rewrite orphaned it. Where the reflog cannot answer (a fresh clone, an expired
reflog), it falls back to a plain merge base and says so. After a
content\-changing rewrite that base sits below the real fork, letting the
span take in commits \fIupstream\fR already has, so the fallback names the
run that states the fork,
.BR "git edit \-\-onto=" \fIupstream\fR " \-\-base=" \fIsha\fR,
\fIsha\fR the newest commit of the span \fIupstream\fR holds a copy of (same
author, date and subject), where there is one.
.B \-\-base
names the commit the branch's own work starts above, a SHA below its tip:
the replay takes what follows it, and the commits up to it stay out, those
\fIupstream\fR holds in no form by patch id listed.
.IP
Before replaying it reports what \fIupstream\fR gained and which of the
branch's commits it already carries by patch id \(em dropped by the replay
however \fIupstream\fR changed them since, their count repeated afterwards.
.IP
Runs in an isolated worktree and CAS\-updates the branch, then brings the
caller's checkout along as after any landing, uncommitted work on a path
\fIupstream\fR changed since the fork included. Each edit, staged or not, merges three\-way
onto what landed from the file's own base; a conflict writes nothing, the
file kept on record and its
.B "git merge\-file"
named.
.IP
Such work is refused before anything moves only where no sync can bring it
along: a checkout halfway through a merge, rebase, cherry\-pick or revert, or
holding unmerged entries. Each path is then named with its next step \(em a
commit where the work is the caller's, else waiting for the session whose it
is, never a stash, which would take every session's work in a shared
checkout. Work on the branch's own files rides along as it is.
.IP
A conflict pauses as usual; where the paused commit is one \fIupstream\fR
already carries in another form,
.B git edit \-\-skip
steps over it. A branch with no commits of its own past the fork moves to
\fIupstream\fR by compare\-and\-swap, journaled, and says so, its checkout
coming along the same way \(em a fast\-forward, unless \fIupstream\fR
rewrote what the branch holds. Only a branch already on top of
\fIupstream\fR ends unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-land=\fIbranch\fR
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-land=\fR\fIbranch\fR \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR      # say what would land, applying nothing
\fBgit edit \-\-land=\fR\fIbranch\fR                # land its commits here, a fast\-forward or a replay
\fBgit edit \-\-land=\fR\fIbranch\fR \fB\-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR   # from the commit \fIbranch\fR forked from, named
\fBgit edit \-\-continue\fR | \fB\-\-skip\fR | \fB\-\-abort\fR  # after a pause
.fi
.IP
Land \fIbranch\fR's commits on the checked\-out branch \(em how sessions
sharing one checkout finish a branch into its dirty
.BR main ,
under the guards every other landing has.
.IP
\fIbranch\fR's own commits are those the checked\-out branch never held: not
reachable from its tip or any value in its reflog. What an
.B \-\-undo
took back is set aside: the commits each land it took back brought, and
their copies a rewrite of the target made inside that land's window. Those
commits are that entry's old..new, whatever the land named, so a land of
.B feat~1
counts for
.BR feat .
A peer's commit made and taken back inside that window stays held. The fork
is the first commit on \fIbranch\fR's first\-parent line with none of
\fIbranch\fR's own commits at or below it.
.IP
So none of these leaves the branch's commits out, nor brings back what the
target dropped: a land taken back, the branch then redone or moved onto the
copies a replay landed, a catch\-up by merge, pull, land or
.BR "update\-ref" ,
or a rewrite of the target below the fork (a reword, a fold, a drop).
.IP
What lands is those commits alone. A commit the target held and dropped
since, which the span carries, stays out of the replay, named, the drop
standing \(em a peer's commit inside an undone land's window, say, even
between \fIbranch\fR's own.
.IP
A commit that is the target's by a land's own record stays out as well:
either a copy a land of the target into \fIbranch\fR made, by \fIbranch\fR's
reflog, or a commit of another branch whose change and message the tip holds
in a copy a land of that branch brought. Each commit the target holds or held
pairs with one copy of the first kind at most, by the author name, email and
date a replay keeps, failing that by its whole message after git's comment
and whitespace cleanup, as a resolution committed by hand at a stop keeps it
\(em so a commit made in that land's pause stays \fIbranch\fR's own. A
fast\-forward happens only where nothing stays out.
.IP
Inside a merge no replay can leave such a commit out, so a span carrying one
there, by a catch\-up merge say, refuses, naming the commit (as rewritten
since where the tip holds a commit of its author, date and subject or patch),
the
.B \-\-base
past the merge that lands what follows and the commits of \fIbranch\fR's own
that leaves out. Where it fast\-forwards, it names the
.B \-\-base
landing them back all the same. It never names a merge, nor a
.B \-\-base
that ends in the merge refusal.
.IP
Whether one of \fIbranch\fR's own commits landed is never guessed: one that
looks like a copy of a commit the target holds or held refuses the whole
land, landing and leaving out nothing. A lookalike shares author name, email
and author date, and subject or patch, as a replay, a pick or a rewrite
leaves one.
.IP
The refusal names each such commit with its lookalike and how the two
relate: on the target with its change and message, or its change alone; its
change as the target first took it, the target's copy changed since; its
change on the target already in another form; as a land of \fIbranch\fR
took it, the difference that land's conflict resolution; of another patch
than the lookalike as the target first took it; or like one the target
dropped since. Then it names each step that lands as printed, one for each
that needs one.
.IP
A commit counts as taken by a land of \fIbranch\fR where the branch held
that very commit when that land started. Its reflog tells, read from the
earliest copy the land made where that first pick needed no resolution, else
from the target's move before it, as a stop's copy is made only once it
resumes. A land under a name \fIbranch\fR had before a rename counts too.
.IP
Where the tip lacks its change, the step is the
.B "\-\-amend\-into=\fIlookalike\fP \-\-tree=\fIcommit\fP"
that folds that change alone, composed on the target's tip, into the copy
there. A copy the target reworded or folded into since is found by the
lookalike's own subject or patch. Only the first such fold is given, as each
moves the tip the next is composed on. Where the fold does not apply, the
step is the
.B "git range\-diff"
to make it by hand.
.IP
Neither is given for a copy a land of \fIbranch\fR brought whose difference
the branch's reflog cannot tell from that land's resolution \(em where it does
not reach back past that land's start, as after an expiry or the branch
deleted and made anew, or where a value of it in that very second changes the
answer. Such a copy is named as such, with the
.B \-\-base
past those copies, which lands what follows them and ends with nothing to
land where nothing does. A difference of its own is left to fold by hand.
.IP
Where the tip lacks the message, the step is the
.B "\-M \-\-subject=\fIsubject\fP \fIlookalike\fP"
that gives the copy \fIbranch\fR's subject where the rest stands, else the
.BR "\-M \-\-text=""$(git log \-1 \-\-format=%B \fIcommit\fP)"" \fIlookalike\fP" .
Where the target dropped the lookalike, it is the
.B "\-\-exec \-\- git cherry\-pick"
that lands the commit back whole.
.IP
Where each copy on the tip carries its commit's patch, the step moves
\fIbranch\fR onto the copies, the target's messages standing. From its
checkout in a linked worktree, that is the
.B "git \-C \fIpath\fP edit \-\-onto=\fItarget\fP"
that drops the copies as there already. With nothing else of its own, it is
the
.B "git \-C \fIpath\fP reset \-\-keep"
or
.B "git branch \-f"
a land names anyway. Then comes the
.B \-\-base
past them, past any merge after them and past every commit not
\fIbranch\fR's own (a catch\-up copy, one the target dropped), landing its
own commits that follow. Where the target dropped every lookalike and all
past the fork is \fIbranch\fR's own, the step is the
.B \-\-base
landing them back all the same.
.IP
Its own commits no step lands are named, with the
.B "git \-C \fIpath\fP edit \-d"
that takes the copies of what the target dropped off it first, the drop
standing.
.IP
The commits the target holds already, by ancestry or by patch id, drop out
of the span. Only where the checked\-out branch keeps no reflog does
\fIbranch\fR's own reflog answer, by the newest value in it the target still
holds. Where neither keeps one, the merge base does \(em refused where the
span past it holds commits sharing author and subject with the target's,
copies a rewrite of the target left, naming the
.B \-\-base
for either reading.
.B \-\-base=\fIsha\fR
names the fork outright. The run says which fork it used and how it was
found.
.IP
Where the checked\-out branch's tip still sits below the commits and on their
fork, it fast\-forwards by compare\-and\-swap from the tip it read. Otherwise
the commits replay onto its tip in an isolated worktree, the result landing
by compare\-and\-swap. A conflict pauses as every replay does
.RB ( \-\-continue ,
.BR \-\-skip ,
.BR \-\-abort ).
.IP
A merge commit past the fork refuses, since the replay would flatten it,
naming the
.B "\-\-exec \-\- git merge \-\-no\-ff \-m"
that lands it as a merge where the target still holds the fork. Past a fork
the target rewrote, a merge would bring the old commits along, so none is
offered. A target that moved while the run read it refuses, landing nothing
\(em run it again to land onto the new tip.
.IP
\fIbranch\fR is a branch first, whatever tag or remote\-tracking ref shares
its name. A relative name such as
.B @{\-1}
is taken as the branch it names when the run starts. A name that is only a
tag, a remote\-tracking ref, a commit or a revision such as
.B feat~1
lands that, said as what it resolved to. It never moves either, and no
branch command is offered for it.
.IP
The checkout comes along as after any landing \(em see
.BR "THE CHECKOUT" .
A conflict's merge is printed after the landed commits, right above the
trailer. Where the checkout can't come along, the run names the
.B \-\-carry
that brings it along. Where the edits predate an earlier landing too, that
carry starts from the earlier landing's old tip, so one carry takes in both.
.IP
\fIbranch\fR itself never moves. A fast\-forward leaves it at the new tip;
after a replay it still holds the original commits, which the run names with
the
.B "git branch \-f"
that points it at the copies \(em for a branch checked out in another
worktree, the
.B "git \-C \fIpath\fP reset \-\-keep"
for that checkout instead.
.IP
Three kinds of branch stay as they are, offered neither a move nor a
deletion. One the main worktree has checked out stays, as after a catch\-up
of it from a worktree branch, and the land the other way round is named
instead. So does one checked out nowhere landed into a linked worktree's
branch. So does the repository's default
branch wherever it is checked out
.RB ( init.defaultBranch ,
else a remote's
.BR HEAD ,
else
.B main
or
.BR master ).
.IP
A commit skipped at a stop, or emptied there by the resolution, lands
nowhere, by the replay's own record of what each commit became. A commit made
at a stop then skipped counts as made in the pause. The run names such a
commit, and offers nothing that moves or deletes \fIbranch\fR, the only
branch still holding it. The same goes for commits of its own a
.B \-\-base
leaves out below a fork the target lacks, which the target holds in no form,
then or ever, even where the rest ends already landed. A resumed land counts
apart the commits the caller made in its pause.
.IP
A run that lands nothing at all moves and journals nothing. A \fIbranch\fR
ends
.B "ok \(en \fIref\fP unchanged, already landed"
or
.B "nothing to land"
where the target holds its commits already: by ancestry, by patch id or each
with its change and message, or as a land of it took it, its conflict
resolved. It ends so too where its replay comes out empty commit for commit,
or where it has no commits of its own past its fork. The deletion is offered
only where its commits were found on the target, bar the three kinds above.
.IP
A detached
.BR HEAD ,
the checked\-out branch itself and an unknown name each refuse, naming where
the run can go instead, by
.B "git \-C"
from another checkout. Beside
.BR \-\-undo ,
.B \-\-carry
or
.B \-\-status
it refuses, as does any mode beside those. A land only adds commits, so the
pushed guard has nothing to protect. Where a replay copies commits already
pushed on their own branch, the run says so.
.IP
.B \-\-dry\-run
names the fork and where it came from, says whether it would fast\-forward or
replay and lists the commits, landing nothing, then the command landing them,
its
.B \-\-base
kept. The gate checks the landed
commits \(em a replay's failure pausing as any replay's does, a
fast\-forward's refusing outright as
.BR \-\-exec 's
does.
.B \-C
names the worktree a replay runs in. The journal and reflog name the run
\fBland\fR \fIbranch\fR, and
.B \-\-undo
takes it back, as does the raw
.B "git update\-ref \-m"
printed beside it, which writes the reflog message
.B \-\-undo
would, so a later land reads either as undone \(em that one moving the ref
alone, where
.B \-\-undo
brings the checkout back as it came. A land taken back any other
way stays held: landing a fast\-forward's commits again ends
.BR "nothing to land" ,
and a replay's, like the copies the target dropped, refuses \(em either
naming the
.B \-\-base
that lands them all the same.
.IP
The rule reads the target's reflog, so it reaches as far back as that does:
past git's expiry of unreachable reflog entries
.RB ( gc.reflogExpireUnreachable ,
30 days by default), a commit the target dropped long before is no longer
known as held, and a branch still carrying it can bring it back \(em
.B \-\-dry\-run
lists what would land.
.TP
.B \-\-commit \-\-text \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-commit \-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...                      # files wholly yours, at the tip
\fBgit edit \-\-commit \-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-edits=\fR\fIjson\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...]     # amid another's edits in the same files
\fBgit edit \-\-commit \-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...        # right after \fIsha\fR instead (or \fB\-\-before\fR)
\fBgit edit \-\-commit \-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR \fB\-\-put=\fR\fIpath\fR\fB=\fR\fIfile\fR \fB\-\-base=\fR\fItip\fR  # a file rebuilt from \fBgit show\fR \fItip\fR\fB:\fR\fIpath\fR
\fBgit edit \-\-commit \-\-text\fR '\fBfixup! \fR\fIsubject\fR' \fB\-\-\fR \fIpath\fR...         # a fix for an older commit, \fB\-s=auto\fR squashing it in later
\fBgit edit \-\-commit \-\-text \-\fR ... < \fImsg\-file\fR                      # the message from stdin
.fi
.IP
Commit each \fIpath\fR as it stands in the checkout on top of the tip, as
.B "git commit \-\- <paths>"
does, though only files named one by one, no directory or glob. A new file
needs no
.BR "git add" ,
a deleted one is removed, and a moved one is named at both paths. A
submodule, read from the checkout a directory, records no pointer this way,
so it refuses, naming the
.B \-\-exec
run that commits its pointer (with
.BR \-\-whole ,
the
.B "git add"
and staged fold that do).
.IP
The tree is composed in a private index seeded from
.BR HEAD ,
each file read through its filters as
.B "git add"
reads it, so the shared index is neither read nor written, and a file staged
beside them stays staged. The commit is then made in a temporary worktree and
landed as
.B \-\-exec
lands its result. The repo's hooks run on it whatever
.B \-\-no\-verify
says, which skips
.B edit.verifyCmd
alone. The branch moves only from the tip the files were composed on, and
the journal and reflog name the caller.
.IP
A file\-mode change refuses unless
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
says it is meant. A file carrying a conflict its tip version lacks \(em an
opening and a closing marker line at its
.B conflict\-marker\-size
\(em refuses, whoever left it there. A larger marker size in
.B .gitattributes
makes such lines content, as for
.BR "git diff \-\-check" .
.IP
Where
.B core.ignorecase
says the filesystem ignores case, a new name spelling another in the commit
refuses. A non\-ASCII letter is folded as the filesystem folds it, whatever
the locale. The run passes where it removes the old spelling, which renames:
both names given whole after an
.BR mv ,
or
.B \-\-rm
beside a
.BR \-\-put ,
a directory's by each file under it. A
.B \-\-tree
is held to the same.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-after=\fIsha\fR " or " \-\-before=\fIsha\fR ,
the commit lands right after or before that commit instead of at the tip
\(em built on the tip, then replayed into place in the run's worktree, so the
branch never holds it at the tip and the gate checks it where it lands. A
stop placing it resolves itself where its line replacements make it, as a
move's does, those
.B \-\-edits
gave where it took them \(em a resume, holding no
.BR \-\-edits ,
derives them from the placed commit's change, as a fold's resume does. Any
other conflict pauses as
.B \-\-move
does, with nothing landed, so
.B \-\-abort
leaves the branch as it was. A pushed commit or a merge above the anchor
refuses before anything is built. With
.B \-\-at=\fIcut\fR
it lands as the cut's last commit instead.
.IP
Where a file also holds another caller's uncommitted lines, taking it whole
would take those too, so the inputs compose the commit from the caller's own
changes alone, on the tip's content in a private index, in this order:
.BR \-\-patch
applies hunks split by hand,
.B \-\-put
replaces a file with one rebuilt elsewhere (from a commit, say),
.B \-\-edits
replays replacements made by hand \(em each old text matching exactly once, so
a peer's line inside it refuses rather than merging, and in order, so one
whose match an earlier edit of the same file rewrote refuses, naming that edit
to merge it with \(em and
.B \-\-chmod
and
.B \-\-rm
set a bit and remove a path. Every path an input changes is checked against
other callers' landings as whole files are, as an edit or a patch built from a
checkout file that predates one takes it back all the same \(em or against
.BR \-\-base ,
the commit its content was built on, refused where that path changed since,
never the tip looked up in the same call; a new path takes the mode
.B "git add"
would give the checkout's file. Files named after
.B \-\-
are taken whole beside the inputs, each exactly as
.B "\-\-commit \-\- <paths>"
alone takes it \(em the same checks and guards, a file the checkout lacks
removed, its mode and links read from the checkout, its index entry
re\-synced \(em a path an input names too refusing, naming both.
.B \-\-amend\-into
takes the same inputs, files taken whole beside them after
.B \-\-whole
(its paths alone naming staged changes), and
.B "\-\-commit \-\-tree="\fIcommit\fR
lands a commit composed on the tip by anything else, no paths beside it.
.IP
A fold given as
.BR \-\-edits ,
alone or beside files taken whole, resolves its replay's stops itself: where
the commit a stop builds holds each old text exactly once in every conflicted
file \(em an edited one, under the name it had there, a rename followed \(em
and its parent as replayed differs there from the original by the edits
alone, that file takes the edits in the commit's own content, what that
commit holds with the fold made in it, and the replay goes on, so no later
commit touching the lines beside them pauses it. At a stop where that commit
holds an old text other than once \(em one that changed that text \(em or
another file conflicts, it pauses for
.B \-\-continue
or
.B \-\-abort
as any fold does, and the resume goes on resolving the stops after it the same
way \(em bar one conflicting in a file a resolution by hand staged otherwise
than the edits make it, which stays the resolver's, so no deviation made on
purpose is undone. A replay running clean is the one it always was.
Any other fold \(em of staged changes, of files taken whole
.RB ( \-\-whole ),
of
.BR \-\-patch ,
.B \-\-put
or
.BR \-\-tree ,
or of the files beside
.B \-\-edits
\(em resolves its stops the same way, as does every resume, the edits
derived from the fold's own change: each hunk from the tip's file to the
fold's is an edit, its old lines the old text, matched as whole lines. A line
replaced by one is matched by its changed words too, a word on either side
kept and more where those match more than once, where the line itself is
wrapped otherwise there, never inside a longer line holding it whole. Lines a
hunk only adds go beside the line above them, else the one below, wherever
that line is held exactly once and followed there as below; words a line only
adds go beside the words before them, else after, where those on both sides
match nowhere once. The gate checks a commit resolved either way as a
resolver's own, and where placed lines leave the replay's tip off the fold
applied to the tip, the fold refuses \(em
nothing applied, a first run forgetting what rerere recorded of it, a resume
keeping its pause for
.B \-\-abort
\(em and names the rerun under
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_SELF_RESOLVE=1
that leaves each stop to you. A file the fold adds lines to with neither line
beside them held once on the tip, or changes more than a text file's lines of
\(em adds, removes, renames or gives another mode \(em derives no edit, and a
stop where it conflicts pauses as before, as does one where any edit to a
conflicted file places nowhere. A squash's, a placement's or a move's stop
whose conflicts are each both sides editing different lines, which git refuses
as touching, takes both sides' edits, as each refuses a tip off the one it
began with or built \(em a fold's, a drop's or an edit's pausing as before
\(em bar a line both change, an insertion inside or beside the other's lines,
a side rewriting half the file, a changed line's text held twice in any
version or a line moved within the conflict, in a regular text file ending
on a line end; the gate checks the commit.
Where the target lacks a line the fold replaces \(em held nowhere there, nor
its changed words, as a later commit wrote it \(em no edit resolves the fold's
own step, as that change belongs in the commit writing the line or a later one.
A fold of
.B \-\-edits
refuses before replaying, naming each such edit and that commit; any other
pauses at that step as before, naming the commit and the line, as does a
squash's stop.
.IP
An edit read from a change, its hunk's or the tip's, takes only the lines that
hunk changed, followed into the stop's content through each commit's own
change on the way, never one diff across them: an unchanged line to where it
went, a line a commit rewrote in a run of as many lines to its place in that
run, changed words to where that run's own change to its words took them. A
given text follows its line only through runs of as many lines, and a target
rebuilt from where the file came, as a squash's, from where it came on both
ways. The same text elsewhere \(em a line a later commit wrote anew, words a
neighboring line holds too \(em is never taken for it, nor is a line or word
that another of its text anywhere in the file could as well pair with in a
least change, or one whose candidates take more lines to check than it
writes, or one only that check settles on git before 2.50, or a line beside a
changed one holding all its words, as moving them leaves it, nor any line
across a commit whose change rewrites the file as
.B git diff \-B
reads it, or, in a file below the 400 bytes it never breaks, keeps under a
third of the words before and after, so such a stop pauses. A CRLF line's
words read as its LF twin's. Any other line is followed whatever was added
or removed beside it.
.IP
In a checkout other callers share, a rewrite one of them lands (a drop, a
fold built elsewhere) changes a file at the tip, and a file still edited on
the old content would take that change back if committed whole \(em one
holding a conflict its sync left, read into an editor's buffer before it, or
sitting in a checkout the run couldn't bring along.
.IP
So
.B \-\-commit
refuses a file lacking what another
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
caller's run of the last 7 days landed on it while the tip still carries it.
The test is that merging the landing in would change the file and nothing at
the tip. A merge that only adds a landed block a second time, beside an exact
copy, finds it held \(em unless the file lacks, beside that copy, a line the
block may slide over, as a stale copy one line higher does where it borrows
the closing
.B },
of the entry above. Blank lines lacked
there alone count as the caller's spacing, and lines taken out beyond the
block's reach as the caller's own removal. Beside the file's own version of
the block, edited or stale (that stale copy among them), which content can't
tell apart, it refuses all the same, but names no
carry or merge, as either would double the block: it names the
.B git diff
against the tip to read before passing
.BR \-\-base ,
or for inputs the pairs, hunks or file that rewrite the landed lines.
A carry or sync leaves such a file as it is, recorded as lacking the block.
Where later runs rewrote its lines since, it refuses a file lacking
the change from that run's old tip to the tip, the caller's own runs among
them. It also refuses a file such a run removed, the removal of one it added
or edited, a file written whole in place of one it added, one still under the
mode before a flip of its executable bit, and one lacking the bit it was added
with. A removal passes where the files the commit adds or changes, by any route, gain every line that run added and the tip still
holds, beside the same lines, and none it removed \(em its content moved
elsewhere, though not back to a name that run took away, nor for an empty
file, a binary or a submodule, which have no lines to move. The refusal
names the run and the remedy, its closing line each run's label and range, the
.B \-\-base
opt\-out above the remedy.
.IP
For edits, the remedy is
.B "git edit \-\-carry=<old tip>"
to merge them onto what landed, a rename's too. It starts from the earliest
such run the file lacks, so one carry takes in every run since, an edit a
later run renamed away included, and one made on its old name before a run
renamed it into place, or by that run itself, by case alone too. A file moved
to the new name by hand is first moved back, as the carry takes its edits
from the old name, where nothing stands there, in the checkout or at the tip,
the step checking again as it runs. Otherwise, or for a fold of what is
staged outside a sparse checkout, what landed is merged into the new name
with the
.B git merge\-file
named.
.IP
Where the file holds no edits of its own or is missing one the run added,
the remedy is a
.BR "git restore" .
Where the caller's own file stands in place of one the run added, it is
moving that file aside or merging it by hand. For the bit, it is a
.BR chmod .
A file the run removed, or renamed to a path a later run removed, is to be
left out.
.IP
Runs under the caller's own label never count; for an unlabeled caller, that
is every unlabeled one. Nor do edits made on the landed lines themselves
afterwards \(em a block it inserted read as edited where the file holds each of
its lines, as landed or changed in one place, or most as they landed where the
inputs composed it \(em except in a file that run's sync left on a conflict.
There the edits predate it, and take it back until the file holds what landed or its
owner changed the conflicting lines since. The refusal names the
.B git merge\-file
the sync named. It does the same for edits beside the landed lines there,
which conflict as that sync did. Nor does a run taken
out since: one commit a drop removed, matched by the author date and email
every rewrite keeps, or a run changing a file's content alone where a later
tip held the file as before it again.
.IP
Where a later rewrite replaced that run's tips (a fold below it, say), a
carry from there reads the run as held. So the refusal names the
.B git merge\-file
from the base the file's record names, else from that run's old tip. A
rename of the caller's own made on a file such a run changed refuses too,
naming the
.B git merge\-file
that brings what landed into the new name.
.IP
.B \-\-base
names the commit the files rest on instead, refusing a path whose content or
mode changed since. Give it the tip's SHA where taking a landing back is the
point.
.B "\-\-amend\-into=<sha> \-\-whole"
folds files taken the same way, under the same guard. A fold of what is
staged takes it too, on the paths it changes. A fold counts only the runs at
or below its target, as its replay applies those above it again, a conflict
there pausing. But
.B \-\-amend\-into=auto
beside inputs or
.B \-\-whole
judges every landing, as its target is known only once the content is
composed.
.TP
.B \-\-exec \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-exec \-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...                           # run it at the tip, landing what it commits
\fBgit edit \-\-exec \-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...             # its new commits placed right after \fIsha\fR
\fBgit edit \-\-exec \-\-base=\fR\fIsha\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...  # a history built on tip \fIsha\fR, refused if the branch moved
.fi
.IP
Run any \fIcommand\fR (e.g. a raw
.BR "git rebase" )
inside a fresh temporary worktree; the branch ref is CAS\-updated only if
\fIcommand\fR moved HEAD. The escape hatch for cases the other modes do not
cover. A commit it makes runs the repo's hooks, a relative
.B core.hooksPath
read from the checkout as every mode's worktree reads it.
.IP
\fIcommand\fR runs in the worktree's copy of the directory you run
.B git edit
from, so a relative path names the same file it names in your checkout, and
.B GIT_PREFIX
holds that directory from the top, as git sets it for an alias. Where the tip
lacks that directory, \fIcommand\fR runs at the worktree's top and a note says
so. A program named by a relative path is read from where \fIcommand\fR runs,
as the shell reads it, and one naming nothing there refuses before anything
runs, naming that place \(em and where the tip holds the file, read from where
you stand or else from the top, its name from there. The worktree holds the
tip alone: one that exists only uncommitted in your checkout names the absolute
path to run instead. A \fIcommand\fR that fails in a subdirectory names it.
.IP
.BR \-\-after=\fIsha\fR ", " \-\-before=\fIsha\fR " or " \-\-at=\fIcut\fR
places the commits \fIcommand\fR adds on top there, as
.B \-\-commit
places its commit \(em a \fIcommand\fR that rewrote history instead refuses.
.IP
The pushed guard runs on the result here, since a wrapped \fIcommand\fR's
targets are its own: a remote\-tracking ref that was an ancestor of the old tip
and is not one of the new tip refuses the apply, naming that ref and the SHA
the rewritten history sits at \(em addressable there, to apply deliberately or
to inspect before re\-running under
.BR \-\-allow\-pushed .
.IP
A standing
.B edit.verifyCmd
gates the result too, run in the worktree the \fIcommand\fR worked in. Having
no pause to offer, a failure refuses outright and names the SHA, and
.B \-\-no\-verify
applies it.
.IP
The compare\-and\-swap covers the \fIcommand\fR's own run and nothing
before it. Losing it refuses rather than run the \fIcommand\fR again, which may
do something else the second time. A history built before the run
(elsewhere, then landed with
.BR "git reset \-\-hard" )
says nothing of the tip it was built on, so whatever reached the branch
meanwhile would leave with the rest, the run still reporting
.BR ok .
.IP
A result carrying any commit made before the run, its tip or one below a
commit the \fIcommand\fR added on top, refuses where it takes commits off the
branch, naming the newest of them, unless
.B \-\-base
names the tip it was built on. A descendant of the tip lands as ever, and so
does anything the \fIcommand\fR builds itself. A rebuild that keeps its
commits' original committer dates reads as built before the run, and
.B \-\-base
at the current tip lands it.
.IP
A reset back to an earlier commit refuses the same way, as it drops whatever
sits above that commit by then.
.B \-d
names the commits a drop takes. A result built on such a commit refuses too,
however
.B HEAD
got there: a reset, a checkout or a plumbing
.BR update\-ref ,
a squash by hand included. It is traced back through the \fIcommand\fR's own
reflog to what the result was built on, so passing back through the start
tip on the way changes nothing. A commit it brings in on a parent below the
tip with no step of the \fIcommand\fR's going there (one
.B commit\-tree
built), or on no parent at all, an orphan, refuses the same way.
.IP
A rebase, whose todo lists what it drops, needs no
.BR \-\-base ,
however it logs its steps (a reword or an edit below the tip, a
.B \-\-root
reword), unless it replays nothing, as
.B "git rebase \-\-onto <commit> HEAD"
does, going there as a reset would. Such a refusal names the
.B \-\-base
that lands the result after all, the tip the run started on.
.IP
A result whose messages end in more blank lines than the commits they
replace refuses too, naming them \(em matched by subject, or by author and
author date, which a reword keeps; a commit brought in from elsewhere replaces
nothing and lands as it was. It is the mark of a rebuild loop reading a
message with
.B %B
and handing it to
.B commit\-tree
unchanged, a blank line more per message on every pass. A loop that carries a message byte for byte, or trims it, passes.
.IP
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops at the result: built in the same isolated worktree, it is reported
with the tip it was built on and a
.B "git range\-diff"
to compare the two, and left addressable by SHA with nothing verified or
applied. The last line before the trailer is its landing, pinned to that tip
and refusing if the branch moved since:
.BR "git edit \-\-exec \-\-base=<tip>[ \-\-allow\-pushed] \-\- git reset \-q \-\-hard <sha>" ,
the flag as the dry run had it. Inspect a scripted rewrite this way
rather than by running the script outside
.BR \-\-exec ,
where the closing
.B "reset \-\-hard"
that hands its result back moves the shared checkout's branch and discards
its working tree.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-message
Also edit the commit message after applying changes \(em at a terminal. An
edit without one refuses it, as its pause amends from
.BR \-\-continue ,
which takes the message as
.B \-\-text
instead.
.TP
.B \-\-text \fImsg\fR
Inline message for
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-s / \-S ,
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
or
.BR \-\-commit ,
which requires it (skips the editor).
.B \-\-text \-
reads it from standard input, which a run losing the race can't read again,
so it refuses rather than rerun. With
.BR \-\-amend\-into
it goes up front, not on
.BR \-\-continue :
the reword happens before the replay, so the commits being resolved already
carry it. It replaces the whole message: where a reword or a squash drops a
body the new message does not restate, the run names it and the commit it is
still readable at \(em and where a reword or an edit dropped the body whole
for a subject alone, a
.B \-M \-\-text
command that, run as printed, puts it back under the new subject on the
landed commit, blank lines kept bar trailing ones. A new message with a body
of its own gets no such command.
.TP
.B \-\-subject \fIline\fR
With
.B \-M
or an edit pause's
.BR \-\-continue :
replace the subject alone with \fIline\fR \(em git's subject, the whole first
paragraph up to the first blank line, so
.B line one
and
.B line two
above a blank line are one subject \(em keeping everything from that blank
line on byte for byte: body, trailers,
.B #
lines, line endings and a missing last newline. A message with no body becomes
\fIline\fR and a newline, as
.B \-\-text
ends one. \fIline\fR is trimmed as git trims a message; one holding a line
break, or nothing, refuses, as does
.B \-\-subject
beside
.B \-\-text
or with any other mode \(em one writing a new message, as
.BR \-\-commit ,
.BR \-\-split ,
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
.BR \-s / \-S ,
an implicit squash or a split's
.B \-\-continue
do, takes it whole as
.BR \-\-text .
Where the first paragraph it replaced held several lines, the run names them
and the commit they are still readable at, with the
.B \-\-text
form that keeps them. A subject the commit already has changes nothing:
.B \-M
says the message is unchanged, and a
.B \-\-continue
with no other change refuses, as with nothing to amend.
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-dir\fR[=\fIpath\fR]
Name the worktree an edit, drop or squash, or a land's replay, runs in (default:
.IR <repo> .git\-edit)
in place of a temporary one; it stays after the run for the next to reuse.
.IP
An existing worktree is reused only detached and clean: one holding a branch
or tracked changes refuses, and under a gate so does one holding untracked
files \(em in the default path those are removed instead. It must be a worktree of this
repository, with no rebase, merge, cherry\-pick or revert in progress outside
the default path.
.IP
One run holds it at a time: a second refuses while the first runs, as do a
.B \-\-continue
and an
.B \-\-abort
of the pause there while another run resumes or cancels it. A pause recorded
in that path as a run starts refuses the run once it holds the path, never
reset there, as does one recorded anywhere else in the repository meanwhile
\(em a path the run had just made then goes again, unless a pause there holds
work.
.IP
An existing directory that is no worktree refuses; name one that does not
exist yet, which the run makes. A new one inside a working tree of this
repository refuses unless git ignores that path there.
.IP
A lock whose holder is gone \(em its process ended, or another started since
under its pid, while one another user runs counts as live \(em is broken
through a hard link to it. A break another run left half done is waited out
until its link is 10 s old. Where the filesystem can't make that link, the run
refuses naming the lock file, safe to delete once no run is live there.
.IP
Only an
.B =
directly after the flag separates the value, and a short form runs on into
it:
.B \-Cy
names the path
.IR y .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-pushed
Permit rewriting a commit that exists on a remote\-tracking ref, refused by
default. A landing that takes the upstream's commits off the branch prints the
push that publishes it, its
.B \-\-force\-with\-lease
pinned to the upstream as the rewrite found it \(em a background fetch would
move the remote\-tracking ref under a bare lease, turning it into a plain force.
A
.B \-\-continue
or
.B \-\-undo
checks again at its landing: commits pushed while a pause waited, or since
the run an undo takes back, refuse there too unless it is given \(em a pause
keeps the run's own.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-new\-path
Let
.B \-\-amend\-into
fold a staged path into a commit that predates it, against the refusal
above under
.BR MODES ,
and
.B \-s=auto
a fixup's.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
Let
.B \-\-commit
take, or
.B \-\-amend\-into
fold, a file\-mode change \(em a flipped executable bit, or a link turned file \(em against the
refusal above under
.BR MODES ,
and on
.B \-\-continue
keep one a conflict resolution staged, or one the landing found nothing
asked for.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other\-actor
Let
.B \-\-undo
take back a run, or
.BR \-\-continue ,
.B \-\-skip
and
.B \-\-abort
take on a pause, another
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
caller made \(em a parallel session's, or one of your own under another
label. A resume journals under the pause's own label.
.TP
.B \-\-tree=\fItree\-ish\fR
With
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
fold \fItree\-ish\fR's diff from
.B HEAD
instead of the index's \(em composed apart from the shared index, a commit
pinning the tip it was composed on, a bare tree checked against other
callers' landings; with
.BR \-\-commit ,
commit the tree of a commit composed on the tip; see
.BR MODES .
.TP
.B \-\-edits=\fIfile\fR|\fIjson\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
replay
.B {\(dq<path>\(dq: [[\(dq<old>\(dq, \(dq<new>\(dq], ...]}
onto the tip's text, given as a file or inline, each old text matching exactly
once, in order \(em one whose match an earlier edit of the same file took
refuses, naming that edit to merge it with \(em and a fold of edits, alone or
beside files taken whole, makes them itself at each stop of its replay whose
commit holds every old text once, pausing at each that doesn't; see
.BR MODES .
It is for replacements made by hand: pairs generated from a diff of the
checkout file take its other lines too, a peer's landing in a \fInew\fR text
where the one\-match rule can't see them, while a file rebuilt from the tip
.RB ( "git show \fItip\fB:\fIpath\fR"
plus your changes) goes in as
.BR \-\-put .
Either way the paths are checked against other callers' landings as whole
files are. The text is the one the commit stores: under a clean/smudge filter,
an old text read from the checkout holds the smudged form, refused as such,
while
.B \-\-put
takes the whole file, cleaned as
.B "git add"
cleans it.
.TP
.B \-\-patch=\fIfile\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
apply a patch split by hand onto the tip \(em keep its context, no
.BR \-U0 .
It applies byte for byte, whatever
.B apply.whitespace
or
.B apply.ignoreWhitespace
say.
.TP
.B \-\-put=\fIpath\fR=\fIfile\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
replace \fIpath\fR with \fIfile\fR's content, checked against
.B \-\-base
where given, else against other callers' landings. A link put onto a link
stays one; elsewhere its content is read through, and a file put onto a link
is a type change
.B \-\-allow\-mode\-change
has to allow. Run from a subdirectory, a \fIpath\fR new to the tip whose
name read from the top is a tracked path refuses, naming both readings and the
spelling that means each \(em an absolute \fIpath\fR adds the new file.
.TP
.B \-\-rm=\fIpath\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
remove \fIpath\fR, checked as
.B \-\-put
is.
.TP
.B \-\-chmod=\fIpath\fR=\fB+x\fR|\fB\-x\fR
With
.B \-\-commit
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
give \fIpath\fR that executable bit, whether or not it has it.
.TP
.B \-\-whole
With
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
fold each \fIpath\fR whole from the checkout, as
.B \-\-commit
takes it, rather than what is staged \(em alone or beside the inputs; see
.BR MODES .
.TP
.B \-\-snapshot
With
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
merge the fold into every first\-parent commit from its target up instead of
replaying the commits above it \(em each through
.B git merge\-tree
against the pre\-op tip as base, so a file moved since takes the fold under
its old name, and each commit keeps its author and message. A conflict stops
in a worktree laid out as any merge's, once per distinct content of the
conflicted file: its staged resolution serves every commit carrying the same,
and rerere pre\-fills a hunk already resolved at an earlier stop. Only a
conflict over content resolves this way \(em a rename or deletion meeting the
fold refuses, as does
.BR \-\-text .
A commit the fold leaves empty stays and is named. Needs git 2.40 or later.
.TP
.B \-\-at=\fIcut\fR
With
.BR \-\-commit ,
.B \-\-exec
or
.BR \-\-move ,
in place of
.BR \-\-after :
land the commits as the last ones of the cut that tag \fIcut\fR marks
(see
.BR edit.followTag ),
right after its commit, the tag moving onto the newest of them in the branch's
own ref transaction. A tag moved while the run built refuses it, nothing
applied, and
.B \-\-undo
puts the tag back. It takes only an unsigned tag
.B edit.followTag
names, on the branch, read again after any wait \(em a pause's resume
leaving one moved meanwhile where it stands, saying so, and one it can't move
there refusing the landing.
.TP
.B \-\-base=\fIsha\fR
With
.BR \-\-exec ,
the tip its result was built on, as a SHA \(em a name like
.B HEAD
refuses. Where the
branch has moved past it, the run refuses before the \fIcommand\fR starts,
naming what reached the branch since and the
.B "git rebase \-\-onto"
that carries those onto the result inside the run; see
.BR MODES .
With
.BR \-\-commit ,
.B \-\-whole
or a fold of what is staged, and beside any input \(em
.BR \-\-put ,
.BR \-\-rm ,
.BR \-\-edits ,
.BR \-\-patch ,
.B \-\-chmod
\(em or files taken whole beside them, the commit the files rest on, or were
built from: a path changed between it and the tip refuses, in place of the
check against other callers' landings, and the tip's own SHA takes a landing
back on purpose, as for a fold of a bare
.BR \-\-tree .
A
.B \-\-tree
commit composed on the tip applies to the tip itself, so
.B \-\-base
beside it refuses. With
.BR \-\-land ,
the commit \fIbranch\fR forked from, as a SHA in its history, over the fork
the reflogs name \(em the span is what \fIbranch\fR holds past it and the
target lacks. With
.BR \-\-onto ,
the commit the branch's own work starts above, as a SHA below its tip, over
the fork \fIupstream\fR's reflog names or the merge base it falls back to.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-\-exec ,
build and report the result, and the landing pinned to its base, without
verifying or applying it; with
.BR \-\-land ,
say whether it would fast\-forward or replay, and list the commits; see
.BR MODES .
Any verify flag refuses \(em
.BR \-\-verify ,
.BR \-\-verify\-span ,
.B \-\-no\-verify
or
.B \-\-no\-verify\-span
\(em as the gate belongs to the run that lands the result.
.TP
.B \-\-verify=\fIcmd\fR
Run \fIcmd\fR against what the rewrite built, before the compare\-and\-swap
applies it. Each commit it checks is checked out in the operation's isolated
worktree,
.B GIT_EDIT_VERIFY_COMMIT
naming it. Overrides
.BR edit.verifyCmd .
.IP
By default the check runs at the commits the mode authored (a fold's amended
commit, an edit's); at the first commit it rebuilt; at each commit a move or reorder
puts directly below one that came before it \(em the topmost commit moved
down, or the one a commit moved up lands on, where a commit it jumped over
that needed it still fails; at each commit a conflict resolution wrote, by
hand or by git\-edit itself, bar its whole\-line replacements; and at the new tip.
.IP
On a terminal, or where
.B GIT_EDIT_PROGRESS
is set, each commit is named on a line of its own as its check starts
.RB ( "Checking 2 of 5 \(en <sha> <subject>" ,
or
.B "Passed 2 of 5 on the run that lost the race \(en <sha> <subject>"
for one a re\-run takes over). The command runs under git\-edit's own zsh,
which resets the options and aliases a caller's
.B .zshenv
sets, so an alias defined there does not resolve in it, while a function or
a script does.
.IP
The check runs against the linked environment, the checkout's current
dependencies, at every commit alike. A commit missing a file the command
references \(em one any command token names as a path at the result tip \(em
is skipped with a note rather than failed, so a deep target predating the
test runner verifies what exists, and the tip always runs.
.IP
A failure pauses the operation unapplied, reporting what it can establish
about it. The commit the failing one replaces runs the same check, or for a
new commit its parent, where the operation left that unchanged. Where that
fails too, the failure predates the rewrite, or the check cannot run in that
worktree at all, so it is not blamed on the operation, which pauses all the
same. Where it does not, the check climbs the commits above the failure to the
first one that passes \(em for a fold, the commit carrying what the folded
code now needs earlier \(em budgeted off the failing run's own duration, which
.B edit.verifyBudget
overrides in seconds.
.IP
This is no content\-edit pause: the result is already built, so a resume
refuses while any edit is present in that worktree.
.IP
Files left untracked in the worktree feed the check yet never land, so a
resume with a gate refuses while the worktree holds any not ignored \(em bar
an edit or content split being authored, which takes them into the commit.
.B \-\-exec
removes what its \fIcommand\fR left untracked before its gate runs, keeping
the links standing in for ignored paths. What the check itself leaves is
removed after each run, so no run sees another's.
.IP
.B \-\-continue
re\-verifies with the pause's own command and tier. A
.B \-\-verify
or tier flag on the resume names another instead, which then sticks to the
operation as it would on its first invocation.
.B "\-\-no\-verify \-\-continue"
applies the result anyway, and
.B \-\-abort
cancels with nothing consumed. The line above the pause's trailer names the
repair keeping the rest of the result, every resolution included: apply it
anyway, then
.BI "git edit " sha
on the commit that failed. A resume restores the worktree to the recorded
result where an inspect checkout of one of its commits moved it, and refuses
a worktree on any other commit, an amend of the result say.
.IP
A reword,
.B \-S
and a squash routed to plumbing reuse every tree byte\-for\-byte and run no
verification. An explicit
.B \-\-verify
on one refuses. Every mode that can change a tree does verify, including
.B \-\-exec
and a split, the split in a throwaway worktree. Neither of those two pauses, so each refuses
outright instead: a split re\-derives its halves from its own worktree, and
.B \-\-exec
leaves its built commits addressable by SHA.
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-span
Verify every rebuilt commit instead of just the default tier's \(em the
tier for a span\-wide rewrite. Needs a command from
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR edit.verifyCmd ,
and can stand for a repo through
.BR edit.verifySpan .
Given on
.BR \-\-continue ,
it raises a paused run to this tier for the rest of the operation, and
refuses up front where no command is in hand. The default tier states how
many of the commits it ran were resolved at a stop and how many in between it
left unchecked; the span tier, what it ran beyond the default one and what
the whole check took.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify\-span
Where
.B edit.verifySpan
stands for the repo, step this one run back down to the default tier (see
.BR \-\-verify ),
keeping the check at what the rewrite authored, where
.B \-\-no\-verify
would skip it altogether. A pause keeps the declined tier for its resume, and
on
.B \-\-continue
the flag steps a recorded span down the same way. Refused together with
.BR \-\-verify\-span .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip a configured verification for this run \(em or, on
.BR \-\-continue ,
apply a rewrite whose verification failed.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Auto\-confirm the drop/squash prompt. A squash asks on either route, plumbing
or rebase, and only a terminal run asks at all.
.TP
.B \-\-wait=\fIsecs\fR
How long a run waits for another caller's pause to clear before it refuses
(see
.I git\-edit\-state
under
.BR FILES ).
Without it a run waits up to 90 seconds, checking every 2 seconds, then runs on the
tip the pause's landing left, or refuses, naming the pause's owner and how
long it waited. It prints one line as it starts waiting \(em whose pause it
is, its operation and how long it has stood \(em and one as the pause clears.
A pause another session takes in the meantime is waited out within the same
bound.
.IP
A run waits only once its arguments passed every check, so a bad one refuses
at once. It acts on the commits they named as it started: a
.BR HEAD~1 ,
a branch name, a range or a
.B :/
search is pinned by SHA before the wait, and a commit the landing rewrote
resolves as any stale SHA does \(em taken at its current identity where its
change is intact, else refused, naming its counterpart. An
.B \-\-onto
upstream naming a branch is taken as that branch stands after the wait, any
other, such as
.B main~1
or
.BR main@{1} ,
as it stood when the run began \(em one on the branch's own line that the
landing rewrote resolving as a stale SHA does, so the branch is never
replanted onto the orphaned commit.
.IP
.B \-\-wait=0
refuses at once, leaving another caller's pause to that caller or to a run with a
longer
.BR \-\-wait .
With it, an unlabeled caller, which may take up any pause, is told to
continue or abort one that is its own. A pause of the caller's own (the same
.BR GIT_EDIT_ACTOR ,
or neither labeled) is never waited for, as only its
.B \-\-continue
or
.B \-\-abort
ends it. Nor is one an older git\-edit wrote before pauses recorded their
label, which any caller may resume.
.IP
Only the run's first check waits, before it takes any lock or worktree; a
later one, for a pause recorded while the run went on, refuses as before. A
pause on another branch is waited for only where the run must pause
itself \(em at its conflict or refused gate, within the same bound, its replay
held meanwhile \(em while an edit or a content split, pausing at once, waits
for it at its first check. A run losing the freed slot to another run waiting
at its stop waits on for what is left of the bound. Ctrl\-C or
.B SIGTERM
while waiting stops the run with nothing taken, a replay in a
.B \-C
path taken back. For a longer wait, say
.BR \-\-wait=540 ,
run git\-edit in the background, or in the foreground under a 10\-minute call
timeout. Refused beside
.BR \-\-continue ,
.B \-\-skip
and
.BR \-\-abort ,
which act on the pause itself, and beside the standalone commands.
.TP
.B \-\-skip
With
.B \-\-onto
or
.BR \-\-land ,
resume past the paused commit instead of through it \(em for one the upstream
already carries in another form. Refused for every other operation.
.TP
.B \-h \fR[\fItopic\fR...]
Print the usage block \(em or, given topics, their entries of this page as
plain text, one paragraph a line: a flag, with or without its dashes or a
value (\fBgit edit \-h edits put\fR, \fB\-h M\fR), \fBfold\fR for
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
\fIsha\fR for the plain edit, \fBfixup\fR for
.BR \-s=auto ,
\fBtier\fR for
.BR \-\-verify\-span ,
a CONFIGURATION key, its \fBedit.\fR, case and dashes optional
(\fB\-h worktree\-link\fR), or a section's title (\fB\-h checkout\fR). Every entry naming a flag
as its own prints, else every one mentioning it, else the key's, else the first section
whose title holds a topic of three letters or more; an unknown topic
refuses once the rest printed, naming the sections. A longer entry opens
with its usage lines, the forms it is run in, before saying what it does.
Prefer it to
.B \-\-help
in scripts and agent runs:
.BR git (1)
intercepts that for subcommands, rendering this whole page through
.BR man .
.SH "STANDALONE COMMANDS"
.TP
.B \-\-continue
Resume the paused operation once the resolution is staged in the printed
worktree (a conflict) or authored there (an edit or content split). It takes
.BR \-\-text ,
an edit's
.BR \-\-subject ,
the verify flags, the allow flags and
.B \-y
\(em a \fIcommit\fR or a mode beside it, or beside
.BR \-\-skip ,
refuses, naming what it got.
It acts on the pause as it stands once it holds the pause's worktree: one
another resume took to a later stop meanwhile refuses, kept for the next.
.TP
.B \-\-abort
Cancel a paused operation. The branch never moved and staged changes were
never consumed, so there is nothing to roll back. A
\fIcommit\fR or a mode beside it refuses the same way, while what a resume
takes is let through. It changes nothing while a
resume of the pause runs \(em a resume holds the pause's worktree, as a second
.B \-\-continue
finds too \(em or a landing holds the journal past 60 s, and says so where a
resume landed the operation meanwhile. A resume that died holds nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-undo
Revert the last completed operation's ref move, compare\-and\-swap guarded
(refused if the branch has moved on since). The journal is the repository's,
so its last operation can be a parallel session's on any branch: with
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
set, a run made under another label, or none, is refused too.
.IP
Each tag the run moved under
.B edit.followTag
goes back to where the run found it, an annotated one to its own tag object
\(em compare\-and\-swap from where the run put it, so a tag moved or deleted
since is left so and named.
.IP
A run whose branch already sits where the undo would take it \(em moved back
by hand, say, with the raw
.B "git update\-ref"
a refusal names \(em moves nothing: the undo says so, ends
.BR "ok \(en \fIref\fP unchanged, already at \fIsha\fP" ,
and records the run as undone, so a further
.B \-\-undo
takes back the one before it. A line repeating the branch, old and new tip
of a later one an undo took back, as an older git\-edit journaled a move
another run had journaled from the reflog, is passed over the same way.
.IP
The checkout follows as after any landing, but for what a
.BR \-\-commit ,
a fold or an
.B \-\-exec
that only added commits landed, which may have come from the checkout, so
stays there as uncommitted changes, only its index entries re\-synced. An
.B \-\-exec
that rewrote history is undone as any rewrite is.
.IP
The raw
.B "git update\-ref"
every landing prints beside
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
moves the ref alone, the checkout left as it is. The steps that follow it
print not with the landing but under
.BR \-\-status ,
for the last run, and an
.B \-\-undo
that refuses it or finds it taken back already. The index entries still
holding what the run landed are named with a
.B "git restore \-\-staged"
of exactly those paths, re\-syncing the index alone and a no\-op before that
undo, guarded by a
.B "git diff\-index \-\-cached \-\-exit\-code"
that resets none where one holds other staging since, a peer's say, and lists
those. Where
.B \-\-undo
would bring the files back, the
.B \-\-carry
that does it after is named too.
.IP
Where one of those paths is flagged skip\-worktree or lies outside a sparse
checkout \(em on a git before 2.42, which can't ask its patterns, any path
\(em a restore would miss it or clear its flag, so no such step is printed:
the run says the raw form moves the ref alone and names
.BR "git edit \-\-undo" ,
which re\-syncs them, their flags kept.
.TP
.BR \-\-carry [ =\fIold\-tip\fR ]
.nf
\fBgit edit \-\-carry\fR            # bring the checkout along to every landing it missed
\fBgit edit \-\-carry=\fR\fIold\-tip\fR  # from the rewrite off \fIold\-tip\fR, as a refusal names it
.fi
.IP
Merge the checkout's uncommitted edits onto what a rewrite landed, for each
path it changed whose file holds edits on the pre\-rewrite content. These
are the files a landing that could not bring the checkout along names, or a
sync's conflict left, as taking the rewrite back if committed whole. One run
leaves the checkout at what landed, the caller's edits merged on top.
.IP
A renamed file's edits move to its new path, also where the checkout holds
that path exactly as the rewrite landed it, or as the old one's own file (a
case\-only rename on a filesystem that ignores case). One still unedited at
its old path moves too, taking what landed, so one run brings the checkout to
the new layout.
.IP
Where the rewrite renamed a directory whole \(em every path it tracked gone to
the same place under one new directory, as git's own directory renames read
\(em the untracked files left in it move to the same place under the new one,
never over a file already there, which is named and left. Ignored files stay, the directory's
own
.I .gitignore
counting, and nothing under a symlink is touched. A directory renamed only in
part takes no untracked file along, the ones right in it named as left where
they are. One renamed by case alone, on a filesystem that ignores case, takes
the new spelling.
.IP
Every other path the rewrite changed is brought along as a landing's sync
brings it. A file whose content is exactly what the old tip had takes what
is at the tip, keeping a mode the checkout changed. One the rewrite removed
goes where its content is exactly the old blob, first, so a directory the
rewrite turned into a file makes way for it. One it added that
the checkout lacks is written. Each index entry still as the old tip had it,
or missing for an added one, takes what landed, also where the file holds
what landed already or was carried before. A file deleted in the checkout
stays deleted, and untracked files stay.
.IP
An intent\-to\-add entry where the rewrite adds the file takes what landed,
its file left as it is. A merged file takes the mode that landed, unless the
checkout changed its own. Under
.B core.fileMode=false
that is read as its index entry has it, never by the executable bit. Where
the rewrite changed a
.IR .gitattributes ,
every side merges under the attributes the edits were made under, from git
2.40 \(em an older one merges under the new ones, where a changed
.B eol
reads as a conflict, the edits left as they were.
.IP
Bare, it carries to \fBHEAD\fR from its mark, the tip the checkout was last
brought to, which each sync and carry writes in
.I git\-edit\-brought
in the checkout's git dir \(em so it brings every landing the checkout missed
since, an undo of one too, and each file its record names as left unbrought.
Failing a mark, the rewrite is the branch's last one on record, a
.B \-\-commit
landing aside \(em with no mark at all, one no journaled landing leads on
from, or one written before the checkout's
.B HEAD
moved by other than git\-edit, a branch switch away and back say. A reset to
.BR HEAD ,
a stash or a checkout of the branch it is on moves nothing. With
\fIold\-tip\fR given, the rewrite is the one from \fIold\-tip\fR to
\fBHEAD\fR.
.IP
Only a clean merge that keeps the edits' own lines whole is written, and only
over the file as it was read. Nothing is written through a symlink, wherever
it points.
.IP
Nothing is written outside a sparse checkout either, one with no patterns
file being full, as git reads it. There an entry alone takes what landed, its
skip\-worktree flag kept. A file renamed out of it goes where unedited, while
its edits stay at the old path, named with the
.B git sparse\-checkout add
and
.B \-\-carry
that take them along. An untracked file whose place lies outside it stays,
named. A git before 2.42 can't ask the checkout's patterns, so every file the
rewrite added is left out as maybe outside it, named with the
.B git sparse\-checkout reapply
that writes those inside it. An agent's landing on a git before 2.35 flags
such a file's entry outside it as well, so it never reads as deleted.
.IP
No staging is overwritten or orphaned. A rename whose old path holds staged
changes stays there. A path whose new entry would drop staging in its way
(files staged under it, or a file staged where its directory goes) is left
whole \(em neither file nor entry written, nor an untracked file moved there
\(em and named with the
.B \-\-carry
to run again once that staging is committed or unstaged.
.IP
A conflict writes nothing merged, named with the
.B git merge\-file
that brings the markers in, a renamed file's new path taking what
landed, the edits left untracked at the old one.
.IP
A conflict, a binary, a symlink, a path changed again since, one the rewrite
removed, one under a symlink, a renamed one whose new path the checkout holds
already, a directory standing where a file lands, its files kept, or an
untracked file whose place is taken is named and left as it was, and the run
exits 1, as it does where staging stands in a path's way or an index entry
stays on the old tip, the index locked, or staging put in its way meanwhile.
A locked index's printed step, like every one a run prints for a re\-sync the
lock kept from it, resets only entries still as the run read them, checked by
.BR "git diff\-index \-\-cached \-\-exit\-code" ,
which runs no textconv, and lists any staged anew since, resetting none.
.IP
A checkout halfway through a merge, rebase, cherry\-pick or revert, or a
sequence of them, is refused. A rewrite that took content out of history
hands it back as uncommitted work, which a merge onto the new content takes
out again. So a landing offers this and never runs it.
.IP
A branch lands into a checkout holding uncommitted edits through
.BR \-\-land ,
whose run brings the checkout along itself, and where it can't, names the
bare
.B \-\-carry
that does, from the checkout's mark:
.RS
.PP
.EX
git edit \-\-land=topic
git edit \-\-carry              # as the land names it
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-status
Report the in\-flight operation and whose it is (or the last completed one) in
the trailer grammar \(em for an agent or session re\-entering mid\-operation.
With none in flight, the last run, where it is the caller's, comes with its
undo and the steps its raw
.B "git update\-ref"
form leaves, as under
.BR \-\-undo .
It lists the branch's pending fixups too \(em unpushed \fBfixup!\fR,
\fBamend!\fR and \fBsquash!\fR commits, ten at most, each with who landed it as
a bare
.B \-s=auto
reads it: the caller's own, another label's, an unlabeled run's, or one made
outside git\-edit.
It names a checkout left behind as well \(em one whose last sync stopped
below the tip on another tree, landings since on record, or files a run's
sync left unbrought that still lack what landed \(em with the
.B "git edit \-\-carry"
that brings it along: the bare one from where it was last brought, or one
from a file's recorded base. A base no carry starts from takes the file's own
.B git merge\-file
instead \(em a fold's, recorded on what the fold read, or one past the
landings on record, where a carry would give every edited file that base.
To a
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
caller whose label the pause doesn't carry, it names whose the pause is and
no step to take there, its trailer leaving the pause to that caller. A pause
it can't report in full names the step that works for the caller asking: one
whose worktree is gone, or whose state file records no operation this
version knows \(em cut short, or written by a newer git\-edit \(em is the
owner's, or an unlabeled caller's, to clear with
.BR \-\-abort ,
and another label's to leave, or clear with
.BR "\-\-abort \-\-allow\-other\-actor" ;
one cut short to where it names neither its caller nor its operation counts
as no label's. It also names the temp worktrees no pause names that a killed
run left registered, with the
.B "git worktree remove \-\-force"
\fIpath\fR that removes each, and those no run's mark tells of \(em an older
git\-edit's, or one a live run is making \(em to remove once no run is live in
the repository.
.TP
.BR \-\-selftest [ =\fIids\fR ]
Run the end\-to\-end test suite in a scratch repository. A passing run removes
that repository and every temp worktree anchored to it; a failing run keeps
both and prints the path. The suite lives in the
.I selftest
directory beside this script, a file per scenario, sourced only for this
mode, and drives
.B git\-edit
as a subprocess, so it exercises the shipped behaviour.
Given scenario numbers and ranges, such as
.BR 83\-87,90 ,
it runs those alone \(em an open end running to the suite's own \(em together with any a
scenario names in a
.B # needs
mark, and its trailer says what ran. That is for runs during development \(em
a pick proves only what it ran, and a commit or a release takes the whole
suite.
.TP
.BR \-\-jobs [ =\fIn\fR ]
With
.BR \-\-selftest ,
run the scenarios
.I n
at a time \(em one a core by default. Each runs with the ones its
.B # needs
mark names, in a scratch repository of its own, and their output prints in
suite order under one count and trailer, which ends in
.BI ", " n " jobs"
after the scenarios a pick names, or in
.BI "\(en " n " jobs"
alone. A TERM, INT or HUP stops the units it runs too, the runner exiting as
any run a signal stops does. A scenario that fails here but
passes in a serial run reads state an earlier one left, and its mark should
name that one.
.PP
Each mutating run also sweeps temp worktrees left in
.B TMPDIR
by a killed process, but only where the worktree's gitdir pointer no longer
resolves and it has sat untouched for 24 hours. A still\-registered one goes
only where no pause names it, it was made 10 minutes ago or more, and the run
that made it is provably gone \(em each run marks the worktrees it makes with
its pid and start time, judged as a stale lock is, and one a run resuming the
pause holds counts as in use \(em while one without that mark, or any beside a
pause whose state file names no worktree, is only named, by
.BR \-\-status .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the released version as
.BR "git\-edit <version>" ,
on the line below the banner. It tracks the
.B v<version>
tag the copy ships under, so a checkout following
.B main
reports the last tag cut, not the commits since.
.SH "OUTCOME TRAILER"
Every run ends with a single line, anchored by
.BR "git\-edit:" ,
that a caller can dispatch on with
.BR "grep \-E '^git\-edit: (ok|error|conflict|paused)'" :
.PP
.nf
.RS
git\-edit: ok \(en <ref> moved <old-sha> \(-> <new-sha>[ \(en <sha>'s edits to <path> landed with <sha>[ and <n> more]][ \(en after losing the race[ twice, last] to <who>]
git\-edit: ok \(en <ref> unchanged[, <why>]
git\-edit: ok \(en dry run built <new-sha> on <old-sha>, <ref> unchanged
git\-edit: ok \(en carried <n> path(s) across <old-sha>..<new-sha>
git\-edit: ok \(en no operation in flight
git\-edit: ok \(en selftest <passed>/<total> passed[ \(en <what ran>]
git\-edit: error \(en <reason>
git\-edit: conflict \(en resolve in <worktree> (<files>); then 'git edit --continue' or 'git edit --abort'
git\-edit: paused \(en <operation> <sha> in <worktree>; then 'git edit --continue' or 'git edit --abort'
git\-edit: paused \(en verify failed at <sha> in <worktree>; output in <path>; then 'git edit --continue' (re-verify), '--no-verify --continue' (apply anyway), or 'git edit --abort'
git\-edit: paused \(en mode change at <sha> in <worktree>; then 'git edit --continue' (re-check), '--allow-mode-change --continue' (apply anyway), or 'git edit --abort'
git\-edit: paused \(en <operation> <sha> is <label>'s[ ("<name>")], not yours: leave it to that caller, or wait for it ('--allow-other-actor' takes it on)
git\-edit: paused \(en a pause with no operation on record is <label>'s[ ("<name>")], not yours: leave it to that caller, or clear it with 'git edit --abort --allow-other-actor'
.RE
.fi
.PP
.I <why>
says what left the ref as it was \(em a dry run, a branch already landed or
with nothing to land, a run that landed or replanted nothing, or another run
that moved it meanwhile, with its move.
A move that carried a commit's edits to another commit, the one writing those
lines, names the first of them as rebuilt, and how many more \(em the giving
commit no longer holds that part of its change.
A run that lost the race and ran itself again names who moved the branch,
the last time where it lost twice.
.I <name>
is the
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR_NAME
the pause recorded beside its label, where its caller gave one.
.SH "TERMINAL RUNS"
A person at a terminal gets a prompt where a pause would end the run: Enter
resumes it as
.B git edit \-\-continue
would, staging each conflicted file left without the markers git gave it
\(em one git gave none, a binary's, a side's deletion or a
.B rerere
prefill, only once changed since the prompt came up, or at a second Enter;
Escape twice cancels it as
.B \-\-abort
does; and q leaves it for later, as Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, a closed terminal or a
hangup does \(em the worktree kept with whatever was authored there, for
either command to take up, and a prompt that followed a refusal ending on a
.B paused
trailer rather than that refusal's error. An arrow or function key counts as
no answer, a key typed before a prompt showed answers none, and a prompt whose
pause another run ended, resumed on to a later stop, or took the slot from
meanwhile acts on nothing. The first known GUI editor among git's editor
sources
.RB ( GIT_EDITOR ,
.BR core.editor ,
.BR VISUAL ,
.BR EDITOR )
\(em Sublime Text, VS Code, Cursor, Zed, a JetBrains IDE and others \(em opens
the pause's worktree, and Space opens it again. Every editor starts in the
caller's own environment, git\-edit's config pins out of it, and one quit with
an error abandons what it was opened for. A message editor
.RB ( \-M ,
.B \-S \-m
and
.BR "\-s \-m" )
runs through
.BR sh (1)
as git runs one, and cleans up as
.B "git commit"
would: under
.BR commit.cleanup 's
mode \(em strip by default,
.BR whitespace ,
.B verbatim
or
.B scissors
\(em its help worded for it, and under
.B core.commentChar=auto
with a comment character no line of the message starts with.
.SH "THE CHECKOUT"
Once a run lands \(em any mode, an undo too, an agent's as a person's at a
terminal \(em it brings the checkout it started in along. Each file the move changed and the checkout left alone takes what landed,
through git's two\-tree merge. The merge runs under the index lock, on what
it checks there to be as it read it.
.PP
Uncommitted edits on such a file merge onto what landed, staged where the
file was staged whole. A file the merge leaves as it is \(em edits made on
what landed, or a change landed through the checkout first \(em is never
written, its index entry alone taking what landed.
.PP
A merge that conflicts writes nothing, since the edits may be another
session's. It leaves them as
changes to what landed, naming the
.B git merge\-file
that brings the markers in \(em for the caller to run at a terminal once it
means to resolve them, in an agent's run for whoever owns the edits. Another
session's are left to it, as its next commit of the file that would take what
landed back, by any route, is refused, naming that merge.
.PP
A file the run itself took whole, or folded as staged, or put, edited or
patched where the file holds what it committed, holds the caller's own later
edits instead, merged cleanly or not, its index entry alone taking what
landed, nothing offered. Only what the commit's hooks or a fold's replay made
of it, and a landing the checkout never took, merge in. A replay's change
that conflicts writes nothing, named with its merge.
.PP
An untracked or ignored file standing where a file lands stays, the index
taking what landed, so it shows as a change to that. A directory there, or a
symlink or a file of the caller's where a directory goes, stays too, named,
with no restore offered.
.PP
A file the run took whole or added intent\-to\-add has its staging
superseded, as
.B "git commit \-\- <paths>"
has it. An entry a peer stages as the sync runs stays staged, named as such,
among the merged files where its file merged. Staging in the way of an entry
that landed \(em files staged under a path where a file lands, or a file
staged where a directory goes \(em stays staged too, whether staged as the sync
runs or before, the path left as it was and named, never as come along. A file a peer writes between the sync's read and its write stays as
written, named as changed while it ran.
.PP
A renamed file's edits follow it to the new path, its destination landing on
its own where they can't. Edits on a file the move removed stay, untracked.
So do edits on one renamed out of a sparse checkout, whose new path takes its
entry alone, named with the
.B \-\-carry
that takes them along once the checkout takes that path in.
.PP
Untracked files in a directory the move renamed whole go along, or stay, as
.B \-\-carry
takes them. A file a peer writes where the move adds one, as
the sync runs, stays as a change to what landed.
.PP
A merged file's mode comes from its index entry where
.B core.fileMode
is off, and a directory renamed by case alone takes the new spelling. A file
no text merge takes, a binary or a link, keeps the edits as changes to what
landed, never as a staged revert. A removal staged by hand stays staged. Each
case is named, none as the caller's. A file staged and edited both is left as
it was, named with the
.B \-\-carry
that whoever staged it runs to bring what landed into the file before staging
their part again.
.PP
What the move took out of the checkout (a file it removed, or what a drop
held) is named with the way back:
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
while it is the last run, else
.BR "git show \fIold\-tip\fB:\fIpath\fR" .
Only the latter is given after a
.BR \-\-commit ,
a fold or an
.B \-\-exec
only adding commits, whose undo leaves what it landed as uncommitted changes.
A branch checked out twice, by
.BR "git worktree add \-f" ,
has its other checkout named with the
.B "git \-C \fIthere\fB edit \-\-carry"
that brings it along.
.PP
Each run holds its checkout's sync from just before its ref move to the end of
that sync, so a run landing a second later waits for it, up to 30 s.
.PP
Where the sync can't run \(em a checkout halfway through a merge, rebase or
cherry\-pick, its index locked by another run past the wait, the other run's
sync held past its wait, or the branch checked out elsewhere than the run
started, a resume run from inside the pause's worktree say \(em the index
entries the move stranded are re\-synced, the files still holding the content
before it named, and the bare
.B "git edit \-\-carry"
named that brings the rest along once it can, from the checkout's mark, never a
.B git restore
or
.BR "git clean" ,
which run later would take an edit another session made since; an index git
can't write as the sync runs is named with that carry too. A run stopped by a
second signal past its move, or killed there and journaled by a later run,
names that carry as well, and the next landing's sync brings along what the
landings its checkout missed changed, from the mark. A carry partway through a
rebase refuses, naming the rebase, its branch and the step it stopped at, and
the
.B "git rebase \-\-continue"
or
.B \-\-abort
that ends it.
.PP
The sync and
.B \-\-carry
are one implementation, and each file comes along from the tip it was last
brought to, not from one base for every file: a file still as an earlier tip
had it takes what landed; one holding a landing's change merges from that
landing's new tip, so a line a later landing took back leaves it; one lacking a
landing merges from that landing's old tip, never taking it back as an
uncommitted edit; and a file moved to its new name by hand takes its edits from
the old name. The landings journaled on the branch in the last 7 days tell
this, down to the tip the checkout was last brought to \(em below it, a file
holding what came before a landing holds an edit, never a stale file \(em and a
path a sync left as it was \(em a conflict, staging, no sync at all, or a file
the landings show stale where a sync found no mark, as in a checkout an older
git\-edit left \(em is recorded with the tip it still sits on, in
.I git\-edit\-unbrought
in the checkout's git dir, until a later sync or carry brings it along. Where
nothing tells \(em a peer's staging on the path, no journal, a binary \(em the
file merges from the one base the carry was named with. A carry over thousands
of files takes seconds: every file still as its base had it goes in one
two\-tree merge, only edited ones merged one by one.
.PP
What a run leaves to do \(em a conflict's merge, or that carry \(em prints last,
right above the trailer, after a land's landed commits. Where a commit line
must stay among the last three \(em a
.BR \-\-commit ,
a fold, a drop, a split, an edit \(em it prints right above that line instead,
and one line right above the trailer names it.
.SH "CONFLICT RESOLUTION"
When a rebase\- or autosquash\-based mode hits a conflict it pauses (exit
status 2), prints the temporary worktree, the conflicted files, the failing
step, and how many steps remain, and emits a
.B conflict
trailer. Every remaining step that touches a conflicted file is named under
that file.
.B git edit \-\-status
lists every remaining step.
.PP
Resolve the files in that worktree, stage them, and run
.B git edit \-\-continue
(repeat if the conflict cascades), or
.B git edit \-\-abort
to cancel. The resume stages each file whose conflict markers are gone
itself, a deletion too, as Enter at a terminal's prompt does. One git gave no
markers, a side's deletion or a binary, stays unstaged, the refusal naming the
.B "git \-C \fIworktree\fB add"
or
.B rm
that stages it as it stands.
.PP
A pause with no conflicted files means the current step became empty (its
changes are already upstream);
.B git edit \-\-continue
drops that commit, as does a resolution staged empty. A commit lost by hand
\(em taken out of the rebase's todo, or stepped over with a raw
.B "git rebase \-\-skip"
whose change the replay does not already hold \(em refuses the resume
instead, named, landing nothing, and
.B \-\-status
gives the same verdict.
.B git edit \-\-skip
is the way to leave one out of a replant or a land, and elsewhere the run
starts again. Before git 2.40 whether the replay holds that change is read
path by path, following no rename, so one held only under a renamed path, or
in a link or a binary changed beside it, refuses too. A change left unstaged
at a pause with no conflicted files refuses the continue instead, as git
resumes from staged content only, the pause naming those files with the
.B "git add"
and
.B "git restore"
that clear it.
.PP
A file whose committed bytes its attributes would store otherwise \(em one
committed with CRLF line ends under
.BR eol=lf ,
say \(em reads as modified wherever git has just written it, though it is
nobody's change. A run settles such a file in its worktrees as it starts,
pauses and resumes, steps its replay past git's refusals over it, keeps it
out of an edit's or a split's commit, and reads it in the checkout, its sync
and hints and a replant's guard alike, as the content it holds \(em never
rewriting it there, so a replant's checkout update refuses over one, naming
it.
.PP
A resume takes the rebase as it finds it in the worktree: continued there by
hand to its end, its result lands, judged as one the run continued itself;
quit or aborted there by hand, it lands nothing \(em
.B \-\-continue
refuses, naming the picks the result lacks, a pick git left out as emptied
counting as made,
.B \-\-status
gives a pause aborted there the same verdict, and
.B \-\-abort
clears the pause. An edit keeps its amend in the pause, so for one whose
replay was aborted there both name the way on instead: reset the worktree to
that amend, then
.BR \-\-continue .
A pause whose worktree is no longer a checkout of this repository resumes
nothing, and
.B \-\-abort
leaves whatever stands at its path, naming it. Every stop a rebase continued
there by hand passed counts as one the run paused at, for the guards below.
A rebase begun there by hand, rather than the run's own, is
refused in progress, and a replant finished by hand on any base but the
upstream it named lands nothing. Conflict markers are refused in a commit made
at the stop, or committed by a rebase continued or finished there by hand, as
in a staged file \(em the base marker a
.B diff3
or
.B zdiff3
conflict writes included \(em and the conflicted commit keeps its own
message byte for byte, a
.BR # \-led
line and trailing whitespace too, as does every commit a rebase\-based mode
replays.
.PP
Each stop names the commit it builds (for a fold, the target itself). Its
resolution is that commit's content, never the later steps' result folded in
early \(em they replay their own changes on top.
.PP
A resolution taking in what a later commit changes \(em the file as the tip
holds it, say \(em leaves the tip the same but that commit's change emptied
into the stop's. The landing refuses it, naming each later commit that
replayed without lines of its own change to a file a stop's commit changes,
where most of those lines now sit in that stop's commit \(em whether the later
commit conflicted there or the resolution staged it beside.
.PP
The stop refuses it first. A
.B \-\-continue
whose staged resolution changes lines a later pick of the replay changes,
beyond what the stop's own commits change, refuses with the pause kept,
naming each such commit with its file and a line, so the stop is resolved
again before any stop after it. It counts as the landing does, a rewrite the
stop's commit makes again set aside where most of the later change survives,
as is a line the resolution removes and adds back, re-indented or moved,
over the stop's conflicted files and any file its commits never touch. A
.B \-\-continue
again on that same resolution goes on, the landing judging what the later
commit lost.
.PP
That count sets aside blank and punctuation\-only lines, and ones the stop's
own commit undoes, as a line it removes that the later one adds. A line the
later commit removed counts where that commit holds the line its hunk put in
its place, so a later edit of a single line counts whole. It does not count
where that commit, rebuilt, still rewrites the line's place, or makes its
rewrite on a line holding it, moved or extended since \(em its change
rebased onto the stop's rather than taken in. Nor where the stop's commit
only removes and adds back, re\-indented or moved, a line whose text is new
since before the commit it builds \(em one a paused edit wrote, say \(em unless
an earlier stop took that text in. A line that commit, rebuilt, still writes
in another file counts as surviving, moved with its code, and so does a
rewrite it newly makes there putting in and taking out the same words, the
line reworded on the way. At the stop, what its own commits write or rewrite
that way counts as theirs in a file they never touch \(em a rewrite only where
the stop's parent no longer holds the line it rewrote, its code moved. A
partial take counts too, in a reorder or move as in a fold. For a binary
file, what counts is a change gone whole whose very content that commit
holds.
.PP
.B \-\-abort
then a resolution to the commit's own content lands it. So does a commit
placed with
.B \-\-after
or
.B \-\-before
placed after the latest commit named instead, which the refusal names.
Where the placed commit's own change rewrites lines that commit wrote, its
change there emptied, the refusal gives that reason \(em the change is what
the placed one builds on \(em and names the placement past it first. Where
a fold or squash took into its target lines one later commit adds, rewriting
none and keeping the rest of its change, the refusal names their move too,
for a fold that makes the target depend on them: that commit split by
.B \-\-split
into those lines alone, as a
.B fixup!
of the target, then the fold run again with it \(em the split keeps that
commit's tree, so the tip stays as it was. The
replay stays paused as refused, and a
.B \-\-continue
of it refuses the same, so
.B \-\-status
offers
.B \-\-abort
alone.
.PP
Only what the stop's commit changes beyond its own pick and the fold or
fixups in it counts, so a fold whose file a later commit renames, moves or
deletes lands. Each replayed commit is matched to its own pick by git's
record of the replay, which a last step of the rebase saves. A pick git left
out as emptied matches none, however many commits share an author, a second
and a subject, and is refused as lost whole where a stop's commit took in its
change, named
.IR "left out as emptied" .
Lines the rewrite itself took from a later commit, as a drop, an edit or a
replant onto an upstream that already holds them can, turn up in no stop's
commit, so such a run lands.
.PP
A pause an older git\-edit wrote kept no record of its stops. Its resume
records the stop its worktree stands at, so that stop's resolution is checked
alike, while a stop a hand
.B "git rebase \-\-continue"
passed before it goes unchecked.
.PP
A resume whose pause another run ended meanwhile \(em an abort, a new run's
pause in the slot since, even the same run repeated in the same
.B \-C
path \(em lands, notes and clears nothing. An
.B \-\-abort
whose pause was replaced that way cancels nothing of the new one. An abort
meeting a resume as it lands either ends the pause before the move, which the
resume then refuses, or finds it landed and says so.
.PP
If a parallel session moves the branch while you resolve, the
.B \-\-continue
refuses as it starts, before it replays anything, naming who moved the
branch \(em or, where the move comes while it replays, before its gate or at
its compare\-and\-swap \(em with nothing applied, the worktree and its
resolution kept and named in the error. Redo the operation against the new
tip after
.BR "git edit \-\-abort" .
An edit or a content split reads the branch again at its
.B \-\-continue
instead, so it builds on the new tip. See
.B Many sessions at once
under
.BR SCRIPTING .
.PP
Every conflict\-capable rebase runs with
.B rerere
enabled, from the step that conflicts through the one that resolves it, so a
resolution is recorded even when the caller has
.B rerere.enabled
unset globally. A later re\-conflict of the same hunk \(em a cascade, a retry,
or a second rewrite over the same lines \(em still pauses, but the file arrives
carrying the recorded resolution instead of markers, so it only has to be
staged.
.PP
Recorded resolutions persist once the operation completes, bar the one
recorded at the stop named where a squash's tip refused its replay.
An abort or an
error that leaves fresh ones behind says so and names them, as they would
pre\-fill a retry's conflicts marker\-free. With
.B rerere.enabled
explicitly
.BR false ,
the recording still runs but is scoped to the operation: the run's records are forgotten when it
ends, completed and aborted alike. A record another
run may have made \(em one live beside it, or the pause in the slot \(em is left
for that run's own end, so overlapping runs never forget each other's.
.SH SCRIPTING
Notes for driving
.B git edit
from a script or an agent loop \(em what the machine\-readable surface
guarantees, and the loop that consumes it safely.
.SS Reading a run
Dispatch on the trailer (see
.BR "OUTCOME TRAILER" ),
not on an exit status a pipeline may have replaced with its last command's.
And keep more than the trailer: under
.B "| tail \-3"
only the trailer and the touched\-commit line above it hold their positions
\(em the summary lines above those (an edit's net history change, a fold's
commits a replay dropped as empty, counted and named, a tip differing from
the staged result) grow with the commit, so a wrapper that forwards only the
last line drops them.
.SS The resolve loop
The safe shape for resolving a
.B conflict
pause externally:
.IP 1. 4
Take the worktree path from the trailer, and list what needs resolving from
the index \(em
.B "git \-C <worktree> \-c core.quotePath=false diff \-\-name\-only \-\-diff\-filter=U"
\(em which names a non\-ASCII file as
.B git add
takes it \(em never by grepping for markers (see the pause states below).
.IP 2. 4
Resolve one file at a time, and validate each result before staging it:
parse it, run the test that covers it, compare its row or line count against
both parents. \(lqNo markers left\(rq is not validation \(em
.B \-\-continue
refuses staged
.B <<<<<<<
blocks on its own, but semantically wrong content passes every marker check.
.B \-\-verify
runs this class of check for you, against the whole built result before it
applies (see
.BR OPTIONS ).
.IP 3. 4
Stage each file singly
.RB ( "git \-C <worktree> add <file>" ),
never a blanket
.B "add \-A"
chained into
.B \-\-continue
in one command \(em that stages whatever a broken resolver left behind.
.IP 4. 4
.B "git edit \-\-continue"
and dispatch on the fresh trailer; a cascade repeats the loop.
.SS Pauses without markers
A marker grep conflates pause states that need different responses \(em
dispatch on the trailer first, then on the unmerged list from step 1:
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B paused
trailer naming a verify failure: nothing is unmerged or to be resolved \(em
the result is built and the caller's own check rejected it.
Editing that worktree is refused rather than folded in; apply the result with
.B "\-\-no\-verify \-\-continue"
and repair the named commit with its own
.B git edit
pass, or
.BR \-\-abort .
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B conflict
trailer with no unmerged files at all: the current step became empty \(em the
emptied\-step case under
.BR "CONFLICT RESOLUTION" ,
nothing to resolve.
.IP \(bu 3
An unmerged add/add or modify/delete: git may place one side's file whole
(or none at all), with no textual merge to mark \(em the file reads as
finished and is not.
.IP \(bu 3
A recorded resolution replayed by
.BR rerere :
the file arrives pre\-filled and merely needs staging \(em but validate it
first (below).
.PP
The pause output flags such files itself, with a
.B "No conflict markers in"
line naming them.
.SS Recorded resolutions
.B rerere
replays a recorded resolution marker\-free into every later conflict with
the same preimage, an unrelated future rewrite's too, so step 2's validation
applies to a pre\-filled file exactly as to a fresh resolution: a wrong
resolution, recorded once, replays wrong every time after, and nothing flags
it.
.SS Span campaigns
A mechanical change to many commits \(em a format migration, a normalizer
sweep \(em should not run as one fold or edit per commit: each replays every
descendant as a diff and re\-derives the same conflicts. Map snapshots
instead, inside
.BR \-\-exec :
.PP
.nf
.RS
git edit \-\-exec \-\- zsh \-c '
  NEW=<base>
  for C in $(git rev\-list \-\-reverse <base>..HEAD); do
    git checkout \-qf $C && git clean \-qfd
    transform .          # the pure per\-snapshot rewrite
    git add \-A
    NEW=$(git commit\-tree $(git write\-tree) \-p $NEW \\
          \-m "$(git log \-1 \-\-format=%B $C)")
  done
  git reset \-q \-\-hard $NEW'
.RE
.fi
.PP
The transform applies to each commit's snapshot, so no step can conflict,
and because the script ends with HEAD moved,
.B \-\-exec
supplies the compare\-and\-swap, the pushed guard, the journal entry and the
gate around it \(em though where
.B edit.verifySpan
stands for the repo, that gate runs once per commit the sweep built, so a long
one wants
.BR \-\-no\-verify\-span ,
a cheaper
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR \-\-no\-verify .
This is a sketch: a real loop also carries each commit's author and author
date onto
.B commit\-tree
.RB ( GIT_AUTHOR_NAME / EMAIL / DATE )
and its message as the shell's
.B $(...)
reads it, trailing newlines trimmed, or byte for byte from
.B "git cat\-file commit"
\(em ported to a language that keeps them,
.B %B
gains a blank line per pass, which the landing refuses. It leaves
the committer date to the run, since a result carrying commits
dated before it lands only under
.BR \-\-base ,
and stops at merge commits rather than flattening them. Conflict\-freedom
cuts the other way too: a buggy transform lands on every commit uniformly
and silently, so sweep the result before trusting it \(em run the tests per
rebuilt commit, or diff each rebuilt tree against its source.
.B \-\-dry\-run
leaves the result unapplied for that sweep and prints the command that
lands it.
.PP
A hand\-authored fold stays on
.B \-\-amend\-into
\(em its replay itself resolves each stop a later commit makes, wherever the
fold's own line replacements apply on the lines its hunks changed. A stop left
over needs a judgement: resolve it by hand, or
.B \-\-abort
and fold with
.B \-\-snapshot
instead, which stops once per distinct content of the file but resolves none
itself. Prove the tip against the result intended,
and read each patch the range\-diff marks changed beyond the targets rather than
counting them: each is a later commit the fold's text reached.
.SS Many sessions at once
Every run lands by compare\-and\-swap, so where many sessions rewrite one
branch, some lose the race. A run asks whether it can still land wherever it
would invest more \(em before it writes a pause, before its gate, as a
.B \-\-continue
starts, and right before its compare\-and\-swap \(em and stops there with
nothing applied once its branch moved off the tip it read, or another run
paused on that branch since it started. Its gate asks again between its
checks whether the branch moved. The refusal names who moved the
branch: a git\-edit run by its operation and
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
label, or a commit outside git\-edit. The run then runs itself again on the
new tip, up to twice, naming each loss and re\-run as it goes and ending in
one trailer \(em wherever a fresh run composes it all again from the same
arguments: nothing applied, no pause taken up, and no message typed or read
from standard input, so never an
.BR \-\-exec ,
a
.B \-\-continue
or a
.BR "\-\-text \-" .
A re\-run takes over each check its lost run passed on a commit it rebuilds
alike \(em the same tree, parents' trees, author and message under the same
command \(em and its verification line counts them.
Past the last try the refusal stands, its trailer saying nothing was applied,
so run it again;
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_RERUN
keeps every loss a refusal. A pause wins over a run in flight, whose
refusal names the rerun with
.B \-\-wait=540
that waits for the pause to clear; a resume refused keeps its
resolution for
.B git edit \-\-abort
and a redo, the step
.B \-\-status
names for it too.
.PP
When many sessions fold fixes into the same deep history at once \(em or one
sweeps fixes into many older commits \(em each fold replays everything above
its target and collides with the next. Land each fix at the tip as its own
commit instead, then have each session squash its own fixes into their targets
once it's done, one session at a time, and run the suite once on the result:
.PP
.nf
.RS
git edit \-\-commit \-\-text "fixup! <target's subject>" \-\- <paths>
git edit \-s=auto
.RE
.fi
.PP
The fixup's gate may wait for the squash
.RB ( \-\-no\-verify ),
and each squash runs gated, one replay taking every fixup of the caller's own
into the target its marker names, another session's left to it;
.B \-\-status
lists every pending one and who landed it. A fix rewording its target lands as
\fBamend! <target's subject>\fR with the new message as its body, which
the squash takes as git's autosquash would. A lost race, or a wait that
ran out, points here when other callers landed on the branch 3 or more
times in the last 10 minutes.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success (including a no\-op that left the ref unchanged).
.TP
.B 1
Error \(em nothing was applied, bar what
.B \-\-carry
names as carried before the paths it left. Every mode but
.BR \-\-version ,
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-selftest
refuses so outside a repository, and from a current directory that is gone
\(em a pause worktree its own
.B \-\-continue
removed, say \(em naming which.
.TP
.B 2
Paused \(em on a conflict, for an edit or content split, at a failed
verify or mode gate, or where a squash's tip refused its replay \(em awaiting
.B \-\-continue
or
.BR \-\-abort .
.TP
.I other
.B \-\-exec
exits with its \fIcommand\fR's own failing status, so a command exiting 2
or 130 returns that too \(em the trailer, an
.BR error ,
tells them apart.
.TP
.BR 129 ", " 130 ", " 143
Stopped by SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM before the branch moved, with an
.B error
trailer naming the signal, cleaned up as a failed run is: its worktree removed
unless a pause it saved still points there, so a
.B \-\-continue
stopped mid\-check stays paused for another. A signal sent to git\-edit alone
takes effect once its gate's running check returns, while one sent to the
whole process group, as a terminal's Ctrl\-C is, ends that check too. SIGPIPE
stops no run: losing the output's reader \(em a GUI quitting mid\-run, a pipe
into
.B head
\(em costs only the output from there, the run landing, pausing or refusing
as it would and cleaning up after.
.IP
Once the branch's
.B update\-ref
has begun, the run finishes instead \(em the journal entry
.B \-\-undo
reads, the rewrite delivery and the index re\-sync all still happen \(em and
exits 0, a line above its
.B ok
trailer naming the signal. A second SIGHUP, SIGINT or SIGTERM stops it from
there \(em one arriving during the
.B update\-ref
once it is through \(em with a trailer naming the move \(em
.B "stopped by SIGTERM after refs/heads/main moved <old-sha> \(-> <new-sha>"
\(em that
.B "git edit \-\-undo"
takes back. First it re\-syncs the index alone for the entries the move
stranded, as its landing would have, and the trailer names them; where the index is locked, it names
the guarded
.B "git diff\-index \-\-cached \-\-exit\-code"
and
.B "git restore \-\-staged"
that re\-sync them once it is free. A first signal sent to the whole process
group still ends the Git command or hook running at that moment, so the run
re\-reads what that command was reading and redoes a re\-sync or report it cut
short; one
ending the
.B update\-ref
itself before the branch moved stops the run as that signal.
.IP
An
.B \-\-abort
finishes likewise once it has cleared the pause \(em its rebase aborted and its
worktree removed, a line above its
.B ok
trailer naming the signal \(em and a second signal stops it with
.BR "stopped by SIGTERM after cancelling the pause (<operation> <sha>)" ,
the worktree removed all the same. A signal at a terminal's prompt leaves the
pause as q does, exiting 2, and one arriving once the trailer is out exits with
the status the trailer stands for. A run stopped while holding a pause it saved
or took up, before landing, keeps it, worktree and all \(em even stopped as the
pause is first saved \(em its trailer adding
.BR "its pause stays, \(aqgit edit \-\-status\(aq shows it" .
.IP
A run killed outright (SIGKILL) once its branch moved leaves the move in the
branch's reflog alone. The next run on that branch, its
.B \-\-status
or
.B \-\-undo
included, journals it from there \(em under the label and caller its reflog
message names, so
.B \-\-undo
takes it back and the whole\-file guards weigh it \(em and re\-syncs the index
entries it stranded, saying so in one line. It does so only where no other
run holds the journal's lock, never waiting for one, never for an
.B undo
move, which
.B \-\-undo
already finds back where it went, nor an older build's
.B abort
move, and only where the run that died held
that lock, as a run of 1.8.0 or later holds it across its move, or once the entry is
15 s old, as a git\-edit before 1.8.0 journals its move up to 5 s after making
it. Killed after journaling its move but before its index re\-sync, a run
leaves that re\-sync to the next run likewise: each landing marks it owed,
then done, in a
.I git\-edit\-resynced
file in the checkout's git dir, and a journaled move at the tip that the mark
names as owed by a run that is gone is re\-synced once, in one line \(em as is
one the mark doesn't name where it names the move before it on that branch
and the run that died held the journal's lock. A mark naming another branch, a
switch in that checkout, or none, proves nothing, so a revert staged by hand
after a landing that finished is never touched, nor after a move by a
git\-edit before 1.8.0, which marks nothing and re\-synced its own.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B edit.worktreeLink
Gitignored paths to symlink from the checkout into every temporary worktree,
one per value (repeatable) \(em a project's checks usually need what the
repository does not track, a
.I node_modules
or a virtualenv, and a fresh worktree has none of it. Links rather than
copies, so a check there cannot diverge from what the checkout has
installed \(em where a commit locks something else,
\fBedit.\fIpath\fB.installCmd\fR checks it on its own install. A pattern
written for a directory
.RI ( node_modules/ )
does not match the symlink standing in for it, so such a link would land
untracked \(em and the amend at
.B \-\-continue
stages the worktree wholesale. That link is not created, and the reason is
reported.
.IP
Link only what is regenerable, never the root of irreplaceable shared data:
anything walking a symlinked root follows it. On BSD/macOS,
.B "rm \-rf <link>/"
with a trailing slash deletes the shared tree behind it, as does an install a
check runs there \(em
.B "npm ci"
empties the checkout's own
.I node_modules
through the link \(em which
\fBedit.\fIpath\fB.installCmd\fR runs safely instead. For a corpus or
fixtures tree, name the specific subdirectory
.RI ( data/corpus
rather than
.IR data )
\(em nested values work, their parent directories created in the worktree.
A value added while an operation is paused reaches the existing worktree on
\fB\-\-continue\fR. A value the checkout has nothing at is named, not skipped
silently. So is one below a path
the commit itself tracks as a symlink or a file: the commit's entry stays, and
a link through it would land outside the worktree.
.TP
.B edit.\fIpath\fB.installCmd\fR, \fBedit.\fIpath\fB.lockfile
How a linked
.I path
is installed, and from what: a command run in the directory holding it, such as
.BR "npm ci \-\-prefer\-offline" ,
and the files beside it that it installs from, such as
.IR package\-lock.json ,
one per value (repeatable). With both set, a check at a commit whose lockfile differs from the one
beside the link's target runs on that commit's own install, and one failing on
the link reruns on an install before it counts, as the checkout's install may
lag its lockfile. An install is kept while the commits checked next lock the
same, replaces the link rather than writing through it, and gives way to it
again when the check ends. An install that fails, or leaves nothing at
.I path
or nothing ignored there, which the amend at
.B \-\-continue
would commit, leaves the rest of that check on the link, said once per
lockfile. A
.I path
leaving the worktree is refused.
Where a path is not linked, or has only one of the two set, nothing is
installed, and the check says so. A pause whose worktree locks other than what
the link was installed from says so too, as a check run there gets the link
and the install command would reach through it \(em at a verify pause, for the
commit that failed.
.TP
.B edit.verifyCmd
Standing verification command, applied as if every operation passed
.BR \-\-verify=\fIcmd\fR .
Pair it with
.B edit.worktreeLink
so the isolated worktree holds what the check needs.
.TP
.B edit.verifySpan
Boolean. Runs every verification at the span tier, as if each operation
passed
.BR \-\-verify\-span .
For a repo whose history is kept green commit by commit \(em where it is
not, the tier reports breakage the rewrite never caused. With no command
configured it raises nothing rather than refusing the run. Each run then reports what the tier cost beyond the default
one, and
.B \-\-no\-verify\-span
steps a single run back down.
.TP
.B edit.verifyBudget
Seconds the post\-failure climb for the first passing commit may spend, zero
to skip it. Unset, it is five times the failing run's own duration
(at least 30s).
.TP
.B edit.followTag
Tags that mark a cut \(em the last commit a release takes, everything below it
shipping with it and everything above it held back \(em and follow a rewrite of
the branch as the cut, one tag name or pattern per value (repeatable), matched as
.B "git tag \-l"
matches one, a
.B *
crossing a
.B /
too \(em
.BR cut\-* ,
say, for a release's cut a session moves on as folds land below it. An empty
value names none. It is meant for local markers: a tag that was pushed stays
on the remote as it was, so moving it there is the caller's own
.BR "git push \-\-force" " \fIremote\fR \fItag\fR" .
Where a fetch maps the remote's tags below
.IR refs/remotes/remote/tags/ ,
the run names that push, otherwise saying nothing. Opting a tag in says it follows the run's branch:
once that branch has moved, each such tag on a commit the run rewrote goes to
where the cut now falls \(em the commit leaving the most commits of the span on
the side of it they were on, which for a fold, reword or drop below it is its
counterpart: what the rewrite made of the commit, or, for a dropped one, the
nearest commit below it the run kept. A reorder or a squash below it leaves it on
top of the commits it held, its own commit moved or folded down or not, and a commit
moved across it is held back or taken in as the move put it, on a tie the cut
staying with its own commit. A commit a run adds or moves right after the cut's
commit stays above the cut, as one landed at the tip does, unless
.B \-\-at
names the cut: then the tag moves onto the newest commit placed. Every mode landing a rewrite moves them,
.B \-\-onto
and
.B \-\-exec
included, a paused one once its
.B \-\-continue
lands; a run aborted or refused its final ref update moves none. Nor does the
rewrite of any other branch or a detached HEAD move a tag the default branch
(where none is found, the main worktree's branch) still holds, which it names
as staying there \(em a trial branch made from the default branch and
rewritten below its cut would otherwise carry the cut off it. One only a backup or another side branch holds follows as
ever. The default branch's rewrite moves its tag all the same.
.IP
Where the run kept no record of the commit \(em a rebase keeps one only
for a
.B post\-rewrite
hook or notes \(em its counterpart is the one commit the run made with its
author name, email and date and its subject, the subject a fold's
.B \-\-text
gave it. A fold into the tagged commit changes its diff but none of these.
Two commits of either span sharing them leave the tag behind, as does a
commit the run never replayed \(em a cherry\-pick on another branch shares
its identity but is never one the run made, told by the branches and remotes
as the run started, which a pause keeps for its resume, so a branch made from
the pause since takes none of it \(em and a match by its change alone, which
can be a commit below the span the run never touched. So do a signed tag, as
a moved copy would lose its signature, a tag of a tag and one whose
counterpart is only a guess by subject or not found at all \(em each tag left
behind named with the command that would re\-point it and why it stayed.
An annotated tag is re\-made pointing at its counterpart, its message and tagger \(em
name, email and date \(em kept as they were. Each move is compare\-and\-swap
from the value the run read before looking up its counterpart, so a tag
another run or a person moved meanwhile stays where they put it, named as
such. A moved tag is named with the local branches still holding its old
commit, which it no longer marks there, and, where its commit no longer holds
a change the run moved or squashed across it or now holds one, with those
changes \(em a
.BR fixup! ,
.B amend!
or
.B squash!
folded below it, what the key is for, stays unnamed. Every move is said,
recorded in
.I git\-edit\-tags
and taken back by
.BR \-\-undo .
A tag no value names stays behind, the hint naming it \(em a release's tag,
say.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set to
.BR 0 ,
.BR false ,
.B no
or
.BR off ,
a
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_*
switch or
.B GIT_EDIT_PROGRESS
counts as unset.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
A label for whoever runs git\-edit \(em an agent session's id, a CI job \(em
closing every reflog message it writes as \fB [\fR\fIlabel\fR\fB]\fR, so a
history viewer can name who moved the branch. The reflog never leaves the
clone, so neither does the label.
.IP
Any character but letters, digits,
.B _
and
.B \-
maps to
.BR _ ,
and the run says so, as two labels may then read as one there. Whose run or pause it is goes by the
label as given: the journal and a pause record a mapped one in
\fB$\(aq\&...\(aq\fR quoting, so two labels mapping alike stay two callers. A
pause or journal line recorded before git\-edit kept it so holds the mapped
label alone, counted as the caller's whose label maps to it, for
.B \-\-undo
too.
.IP
A set label marks the run as an agent's:
no prompt at a pause, at a terminal too, and a merge onto the checkout that
conflicts left to whoever owns the edits, as
.B "THE CHECKOUT"
says \(em the checkout itself is brought along as any run brings it. An undo's
entry names only whoever undid; a pause records its label, which another
label's
.BR \-\-continue ,
.B \-\-skip
or
.B \-\-abort
refuses, as it does a state file cut short to where it names neither its
label nor its operation. The
journal records the label too, for
.B \-\-undo
to refuse a labeled caller another's run, naming the caller's own last run
and, where its branch still stands on it, the
.B "git update\-ref"
its landing printed, which still takes it back \(em and for the
.BR \-\-commit / \-\-whole
landing guard to tell another caller's landing from the caller's own: runs
under the caller's label never count, so for an unlabeled caller every
unlabeled run is its own, and a file may take any of them back.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR_NAME
A name for the
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
label \(em an agent session's title, say \(em which a pause records, so every
message naming its owner shows the name beside the label: to a run the pause
is in the way of, while one waits for it, in
.B \-\-status
and in a refused resume. Any control character becomes a space, keeping it to
one line, and it is cut to 100 characters. A run with no label or a blank name
records none, and a resume keeps the name the pause was taken under.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_WORKTREE_LINK
Colon\-separated paths to link into temporary worktrees, added to whatever
.B edit.worktreeLink
already names.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set (or if stdout is not a terminal, or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ),
output carries no ANSI escapes.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_RESOLVE
If set, disables resolving a stale \fIcommit\fR to its post\-rewrite identity;
every unreachable commit is then refused.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_AUTO_OPEN
If set, does not auto\-open the worktree in a detected GUI editor at
interactive pauses.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_REPLAY
If set, the plumbing modes rebuild descendants one \fBcommit\-tree\fR at a time
instead of in one proven \fBgit replay\fR pass.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_SELF_RESOLVE
If set, the stops of a fold's, a squash's, a placement's, a move's or an
edit's replay all pause for the resolver, none made by its line replacements,
by both sides' edits to touching lines or, a move's, by line identity; a fold's
final step still resolves itself as the staged tree.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_RERUN
If set, a run that lost the race to another caller refuses at once instead of
running itself again on the new tip, up to twice \(em for a script or suite
that pins the refusal, or retries on its own terms. See
.BR "Many sessions at once" .
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_PROGRESS
If set, the verify gate names each commit as its check starts even where
stdout is not a terminal \(em for a caller that shows a long gate's progress.
.TP
.BR GIT_DIR ", " GIT_WORK_TREE ", " GIT_INDEX_FILE ", " GIT_COMMON_DIR ", " GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY
Taken, and dropped, only where they name what the directory finds without
them; otherwise refused, as every git the run's temporary worktrees start would
take them too and work on the caller's checkout. A work tree only
.B GIT_DIR
reaches, as in a dotfiles setup, is told the
.B .git
file that works instead \(em a
.B gitdir:
line naming that repository, as
.B "git init \-\-separate\-git\-dir"
writes, with
.B core.bare
set false where it was true. The file goes at the work tree's top, a path
named only where it is known \(em from
.B GIT_WORK_TREE
or
.B core.worktree
\(em and left for the caller to fill in otherwise, as git then takes the current
directory for the top, which no listing proves, and a
.B .git
file in a subdirectory would have every tracked file read as deleted.
.SH FILES
All four sit in the repository's common git directory, so every worktree
shares them.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-state
The paused operation \(em its worktree, branch, target and phase. Only one can
be paused at a time: while this file exists a mode moving the paused branch
waits for it to clear, up to
.B \-\-wait
(90 seconds by default), then refuses \(em as do an edit and a content
split on any branch, which pause at once, and a
.B \-\-land
of the paused branch or an
.B \-\-onto
replanting onto it.
A run on another branch goes ahead beside the pause, waiting for it only once
it must pause itself \(em at a conflict or a refused gate \(em and refusing
with nothing applied where that wait runs out.
.B \-\-undo
refuses at once where the pause is on the branch it moves, naming whose pause
it is \(em another caller's is left to that caller to continue or abort \(em as
does a run on a pause of the caller's own. A run reaching a pause of its own
after a parallel session's took the slot on its branch refuses without
waiting and removes the temporary worktree it made, nothing there authored
yet \(em as does a run about to gate or land on a branch another run paused on
since it started, the pause winning (see
.B Many sessions at once
under
.BR SCRIPTING ).
An edit, drop or squash asks again once it holds its
.B \-C
path, or before it makes its temporary worktree, so a pause recorded while it
waited or prompted refuses it there, at once.
.B \-\-continue
and
.B \-\-abort
clear it.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-state\-verify
The failing verification's whole output, headed by the command, its exit status
and its duration \(em the pause itself prints only the last 15 lines, and the
trailer names this path for a driver reading through a
.BR "| tail -N" .
It lives as long as the pause.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-journal
One line per completed ref move (\fB<epoch> <ref> <old> <new> <label>\fR,
then a tab and the caller's
.B GIT_EDIT_ACTOR
label as given for a labeled run, in \fB$\(aq\&...\(aq\fR quoting where it
maps), behind
.B \-\-undo
and the last\-operation report of
.BR \-\-status .
Its lock,
.IR git\-edit\-journal.lock ,
held from a branch's
.B update\-ref
until its line is in, and across each write to a pause, names the run holding
it by pid and start time. Another run waits while that run lives, up to 60 s,
then refuses, moving nothing \(em at once where it runs under that run, as a
.B git edit
a landing's hook runs does. A lock whose run is gone is broken at once, one
naming none, as a run killed while writing it leaves, once older than 10 s,
and one naming a pid alone, as an older git\-edit wrote it, once older than
60 s, its pid then taken for another process's. A run that cannot read
its own start time names it
.BR \- ,
held for as long as its pid lives.
.TP
.I <git dir>/git\-edit\-tags
One line per tag a landing moved under
.B edit.followTag
(\fB<epoch> <ref> <old> <new> <tag ref> <from> <to>\fR, the first four the
branch's move as its journal line has them), so
.B \-\-undo
puts each back with the move it followed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Fold staged changes into the commit that introduced the code:
.B git add src/foo.js && git edit \-\-amend\-into=auto
.TP
Reword an earlier commit without touching the working tree:
.B git edit \-M \-\-text="Better subject" abc1234
.TP
Change only its subject, the body kept byte for byte:
.B git edit \-M \-\-subject="Better subject" abc1234
.TP
Split an icons change out of a mixed commit:
.B git edit \-\-split=abc1234 \-\-text="Add icons" \-\- src/svg/
.TP
Split a refactor out of a commit that mixed it into one file (\fB\-\-text\fR names the lower commit):
.B git edit \-\-split=abc1234 \-\-text="Refactor: Rename helpers"
.TP
Land a finished branch into a checkout others' uncommitted work sits in:
.B git edit \-\-land=feature \-\-dry\-run && git edit \-\-land=feature
.TP
Fold with the test suite as the gate:
.B git edit \-\-amend\-into=abc1234 \-\-verify='npm test' \-\- src/foo.js
.TP
Land a branch into a checkout holding uncommitted edits, then, where its
index was locked, bring them along with the carry the land names \(em bare, it
takes in every landing the checkout missed, another session's too:
.B git edit \-\-land=topic
then
.B git edit \-\-carry
.TP
Undo the last operation:
.B git edit \-\-undo
